{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391579": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: SUPP113823 The role: The School of Psychology and Neuroscience is looking to recruit a full-time Administrative Assistant, until 31st March 2027, to provide a professional, efficient and friendly service. You will form part of a happy, dynamic executive support team who work closely together to ensure key School activities are managed and delivered on time. You will provide general office and clerical support in the newly formed School of Psychology and Neuroscience, performing a clearly defined range of standard tasks within established routines and procedures. This is a fixed-term contract until 31st March 2027 What will you be doing?: The Administrative Assistant acts as the first point of contact for most staff from across the University, external contacts and visitors. It is very important to ensure that those visiting the School are made to feel welcome and that their queries have been addressed to a high standard by exhibiting tact, discretion, maintaining confidentiality as required and demonstrating a steady hand regardless of the volume of work. Independence and initiative will be required to react to changing priorities and work circumstances, with scope to make decisions within very clear parameters. The role holder will organise their own day-to-day work to meet clear objectives providing an effective service to others. You should apply if: You should possess a high level of accuracy and flexibility, as well as excellent organisational and customer service skills, as these are all essential attributes for the role. You should be a good team player and also have excellent communication skills as you will be dealing with a wide range of colleagues on a daily basis. In addition, you should be experienced in using a range of IT systems and be able to adapt to new systems and processes quickly. Due to the fixed-term nature of the role, we would particularly welcome applications from candidates who, based on their experience and attitude, can demonstrate that they will hit the ground running in this role. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term until 31/03/2027 Work pattern: Full-time Grade: D Salary: £24,685 - £26,093 per annum School/Unit: School of Psychology and Neuroscience Shift pattern: 35 hours per week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 19th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Isaac Bale - i.bale@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391415": {
      "title": "AI Infrastructure Associate Engineer (3 x FTE)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/01/2027 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113121 The role: The Bristol Centre for Supercomputing (BriCS) runs the Isambard-AI National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource, recently announced AI Data facility and the Isambard 3 Tier-2 Supercomputer. Isambard-AI is the most powerful supercomputer in the UK and amongst the most powerful in Europe. The BriCS team owns the entire process of developing and operating the centre’s compute and software infrastructure, which includes: The sourcing of hardware and system design. The deployment of huge software-defined infrastructure using tools such as Kubernetes and Terraform / OpenTofu. Building and operating platforms to enable researchers to conduct leading-edge research using the systems. Optimising and refining software to ensure environmental and economic efficient use. As one of the largest Open AI Research Resources internationally, we are committed to catalysing an AI transformation in the research and development community. In this role, you will work as part of the BriCS team to build and operate primarily the infrastructure and compute platforms that researchers use for their work. You do not need to be an AI or computational research domain expert to deliver world-class infrastructure, but you do need to quickly obtain a deep technical understanding of new domains. You should enjoy being self-directed and identifying the most important problems to solve as the team matures with standardized tools and processes around stability, robust service delivery and scaling. What will you be doing?: Use tools such as Python, Rust, Terraform / OpenTofu, Kubernetes, Git and Bash. Design and operate large, highly available supercomputing services managed as software-defined infrastructures, and integrated as complete computational experiments. You will experience designing and operating massive-scale GPU and combined CPU/GPU workloads across these services. You will design and debug platforms, and will work closely with researchers as you co-design solutions that will enable the development and operation of new algorithms and software to solve leading-edge research problems. You should apply if: Want to help build, maintain and secure some of the largest, modern software-defined supercomputing systems. Would enjoy working with world class domain and AI researchers as your primary workload . Have supported development or operation of a small to large clusters or dabbled in building your own physical or software-defined systems. Love operating large distributed, highly available systems, and want to see them used for truly open national-scale research in a cybersecurity compliant manner. For our AI Supercomputing Infrastructure Associate Engineer role focusing on storage and networking, you’ll need: Domain knowledge in 1 or more areas from SysOps, NetOps, DevOps, SecOps, MLOps or Research Software Engineering. Degree (or equivalent practical experience) in computer science, computational or ML/AI research or in a natural science with a high degree of competence in computer science or computational research. Ability to contribute as a member of diverse, technical teams and to follow operational procedures. The available job description provides a full view of the person specification. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ For any informal enquiries, please contact Emma Rose, Centre Manager - emma.rose@bristol.ac.uk. Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until August 2030. Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Positions available: x3 FTE Grade: J Salary: £44,746 - £50,253 per annum. School/Unit: Bristol Centre for Supercomputing (BriCS) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 31st January 2027 Please note that applications will be reviewed and shortlisted on a rolling basis throughout the advertising period. Interviews will also be held at regular intervals during this time. We reserve the right to close the advert early once the vacancies have been filled, therefore early applications are encouraged. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391478": {
      "title": "AI Supercomputing Infrastructure Engineer (3 x FTE)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/01/2027 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113120 The role: The Bristol AI Supercomputing Centre runs the Isambard-AI National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource, recently announced AI Data facility and the Isambard3 Tier-2 Supercomputer. Isambard-AI is the most powerful supercomputer in the UK and amongst the most powerful in Europe. The AI Supercomputing team owns the entire process of developing and operating the centre’s compute and software infrastructure, which includes: The sourcing of hardware and system design. The deployment of huge software-defined infrastructure using tools such as Kubernetes and Terraform / OpenTofu. Building and operating platforms to enable researchers to conduct leading-edge research using the systems. Optimising and refining software to ensure environmental and economic efficient use. As one of the largest Open AI Research Resources internationally, we are committed to catalysing an AI transformation in the research and development community. In this role, you will work as part of the AI Supercomputing Team to build and operate primarily the infrastructure and compute platforms that researchers use for their work. You do not need to be an AI or computational research domain expert to deliver world-class infrastructure, but you do need to quickly obtain a deep technical understanding of new domains. You should enjoy being self-directed and identifying the most important problems to solve as the team matures with standardized tools and processes around stability, robust service delivery and scaling. This role supports hybrid working arrangements to provide flexibility. While the Centre's operational needs require successful candidates to start as soon as possible, we are able to accommodate individual notice periods to attract top talent. What will you be doing?: As a member of the AI Supercomputing Team, you will; Use tools such as Python, Rust, Terraform / OpenTofu, Kubernetes, Git and Bash. Design and operate large, highly available supercomputing services managed as software-defined infrastructures, and integrated as complete computational experiments. You will experience designing and operating massive-scale GPU and combined CPU/GPU workloads across these services. You will design and debug platforms, and will work closely with researchers as you co-design solutions that will enable the development and operation of new algorithms and software to solve leading-edge research problems. You should apply if: Want to help build, maintain and secure some of the largest, modern software-defined supercomputing systems. Would enjoy working with world class domain and AI researchers as your primary workload. Have built small to large clusters or dabbled in building your own physical or software-defined systems, and have motivation to scale up to something massive and nationally impactful . Love building large distributed, highly available systems, and want to see them used for truly open national-scale research in a cybersecurity compliant manner. For our AI Supercomputing Infrastructure Engineer role focusing on storage and networking, you’ll need: Domain expertise in 1 or more areas from NetOps and DevOps. Degree (or equivalent practical experience) in computer science, computational or ML/AI research or in a natural science with a high degree of competence in computer science or computational research. Good organisational skills to manage not just your own workload but also that of less experienced members of the team. The available job description provides a full view of the person specification. Additional information: For any informal enquiries, please contact Emma Rose, Centre Manager - emma.rose@bristol.ac.uk. Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until August 2030. Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Positions available: x3 FTE Grade: K Salary: £50,253 - £58,225 per annum. School/Unit: Bristol Centre for Supercomputing (BriCS) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 31st January 2027 Please note that applications will be reviewed and shortlisted on a rolling basis throughout the advertising period. Interviews will also be held at regular intervals during this time. We reserve the right to close the advert early once the vacancies have been filled, therefore early applications are encouraged. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391638": {
      "title": "Catering Supervisor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113847 The role: Source catering is the University of Bristol’s award-winning catering brand and are looking for an enthusiastic and friendly individual to take the role of Catering Supervisor at the university. The role will be working in one of our catering outlets which include 4 catered halls of residence, events & conference spaces, 9 cafes, 2 bars and a food court and will suit someone who has experience in the industry and can demonstrate leadership qualities. This is an amazing opportunity that provides work-life balance, training & development, working alongside a large and supportive team and involvement in exciting food & drink projects. The contract is 35 hours per week, working 5 days out of 7 with a generous amount of time off throughout the year. Our Catering Supervisors will look after daily tasks, ensuring our team have what they need to do their job on shift and setting the tone of friendly and professional customer service What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will focus on leading the front of house catering team members, setting an example of great customer service whilst supporting the managers with some of the daily tasks like ordering, rotas, end of day duties and due diligence. Setting an example, great communication, organisation and a genuine interest in food & drink will be key to your success. You should apply if: Hospitality experience in food service and dealing with guests Supervisory experience Experience of working within a diverse team High organisational skills and the ability to adapt to the needs of the business Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time - 35 hours Shift pattern: 5 days over 7 Grade: D Salary: £24,685 - £26,093 per annum School/Unit: Campus Division - Catering & Events Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Thursday 22nd October For informal queries please contact: Ioana Dobos ( Unit Manager ) – ioana.dobos@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391597": {
      "title": "Cleaner (Langford)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113816 The role: Working early mornings, in the small friendly cleaning team at the Langford Campus, BS40 5DU, (14 miles from central Bristol), you will be responsible for the presentation of a variety of facilities and rooms for students, staff and veterinary services buildings. What will you be doing?: You will undertake cleaning duties such as: dusting, mopping, sweeping, vacuuming, and washroom cleaning within University properties, following departmental procedures that meet specified standards. You should apply if: No experience is necessary as training will be provided. You must be hard working, flexible and have a helpful nature towards customers. You must be able to have transport to work at the Langford Campus, North Somerset for 6am start. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time - 25 hours per week Shift pattern: Early morning start, 6am Grade: B Salary: £24,479 pro rata School/Unit: Campus Division - Site Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Tuesday 20th October For informal queries please contact: Kelsey Hatcher (Cleaning Supervisor) on 07765334312 or kelsey.hatcher@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391655": {
      "title": "Core Facilities Technician (Teaching and Research)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: SUPP113822 The role: We are seeking applications for the role of Core Facilities Technician (Teaching and Research) for the School of Psychology and Neuroscience. You will provide technical support, training and specialist advice to staff and students in Psychology and Neuroscience relating to specialist software, experimental equipment and laboratory safety. You will deliver software and hardware workshops for UG and PGT students and provide day-to-day technical support for teaching and research activities. You will support teaching and research activities in compliance with Health and Safety and regulatory requirements, providing training and guidance on specialised techniques, software and equipment. What will you be doing?: You will: Hold delegated responsibility for elements of Health and Safety such as reviewing student and staff research risk assessments as well as production of up-to-date Standard Operating Procedures under the direction of the Technical Manager to ensure compliance with relevant health and safety requirements. Support the efficient and safe running of the Psychology and Neuroscience research laboratories across the Priory Road complex and Biomedical Sciences Building, including Health and Safety and compliance with the relevant regulatory policies and procedures. Assist in the development, programming and use of specialised equipment, software and processes in research and teaching. Support and/or deliver workshops, troubleshooting sessions and induction talks for UG, PGT and PGR students. Support Widening Participation, community engagement and research events. Undertake or co-ordinate maintaining, upgrading, optimising and integrating of laboratory equipment. Support academic staff in the testing of participants as part of ongoing research or teaching within the School. Support the development of systems and maintain records of laboratory and equipment bookings, and equipment maintenance. Support staff and student IT/ AV queries as required Hold responsibility for staff and student induction into specialised spaces. Undertake any other tasks that may reasonably be requested by the relevant Technical Manager/s or School Head of Operations. You should apply if: You have: Relevant technical skills or work experience in Psychology and Neuroscience, or a related discipline. A demonstrable understanding of Psychology teaching and research laboratory environments. An understanding of qualitative and quantitative research methods in psychology. Experience with research software and laboratory technologies relevant to Psychology and Neuroscience. A proven ability to structure and deliver curriculum support sessions on technical issues. Knowledge of British Psychological Society (BPS) guidelines and procedures for human research. Experience in providing technical support to undergraduate and/or postgraduate students. Knowledge of current health and safety regulations and safe working practices in Psychology and/or neuroscience disciplines. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full-time Grade: F Salary: £28,778 - £32,080 per annum School/Unit: School of Psychology and Neuroscience This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Thursday 22nd October 2026 Interviews are anticipated to be held on Monday 2nd November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Natalie Griffiths-Stubbs, Technical Manager via email: Natalie.griffiths@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391555": {
      "title": "Dental Therapist Tutor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108708 The role: We are seeking exceptional Dental Therapists to join our team and deliver outstanding education and team training to our Dental Hygiene and Therapy students at Bristol Dental School. Working within your Scope of Practice you will be responsible for the delivery of academic and clinical teaching to our undergraduates, working in an integrated teaching environment. What will you be doing?: Part of our education strategy is to have a strong emphasis on integrating learning across all disciplines and team training. The appointee will initially be responsible for the clinical supervision of Dental Therapy (BDT) and Dental Hygiene (BDH) students. For BDT/BDH students this concept starts in the Clinical Skills Simulation Clinic and culminates in clinical placements within the Community and clinics in our purpose-built General Practice Suite alongside Final year BDS students. The appointee will develop other teaching and learning activities, working alongside academic colleagues to deliver an outstanding student experience. You should apply if: The successful candidate would ideally have experience of teaching, this may be at a practice-level, but should be able to demonstrate a passion and enthusiasm for teaching. The appointee will have excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with an ability to foster collaborative approaches across clinical disciplines. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time Grade: J Salary: £44,746 – 50,253 p.a (pro rata) School/Unit: Bristol Dental School Shift pattern: 16 hours per week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 18/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Sarah Bain sarah.bain@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391647": {
      "title": "Deputy Business Development Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Centre for Sport Exercise and Health Reference: SUPP113768 The role: This is a key role within the Business Development Team, supporting the Business Development Manager in leading a centralised team that delivers a wide range of commercial and business services across the division. The team plays a vital role in generating income to support and enhance the student experience, with responsibility for delivering an annual income target of approx. £3 million. It leads on business development activities including marketing and communications, facilities hire and booking management, event delivery, systems development and support, customer relationship management, financial management and administration services. As Deputy Business Development Manager, you will help drive commercial performance, support the development of new opportunities, and ensure the effective delivery of high-quality services. Working closely with colleagues across the division, you will provide leadership, operational oversight and customer-focused support, helping teams achieve their objectives and contribute to the division's overall success. What will you be doing?: As Deputy Business Development Manager, you will lead key areas of the Business Development team's portfolio, with responsibility for income generation, customer management, and systems development. You will identify opportunities for continuous improvement and lead initiatives that enhance service delivery, customer experience, and commercial performance. A significant part of the role will involve leading our Memberships Team, which generates more than half of the division's annual income. You will support the team in achieving ambitious targets, oversee membership growth and retention, and work closely with Front of House colleagues to resolve escalated customer issues and ensure a consistently high-quality customer experience. You will build and maintain strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders to support the management, development, and implementation of divisional systems, ensuring compliance with university policies, procedures, and relevant legislation. Current systems include Legend Leisure Management System (including the Innovatise app), CoursePro, Develop, and MyERP. Using business intelligence and performance data, you will provide insight and recommendations to support decision-making and drive improvements across the division. This may include analysing engagement and participation trends, membership sales performance, facility utilisation, customer behaviour, and progress against income and service delivery KPIs. Working closely with the Business Development Manager, you will help ensure the division achieves its commercial objectives and service standards. You will contribute to strategic planning and policy development where required and provide leadership support by deputising for the Business Development Manager at meetings and key stakeholder forums. You should apply if: Experience of leading, developing, or supervising teams, with a commitment to supporting colleague development and delivering high-quality services. Significant experience working within business development, commercial operations, customer service, membership management, or a related area. Experience of identifying and implementing process improvements, with strong analytical and problem-solving skills. The ability to interpret complex data and translate insights into clear recommendations for a range of audiences. Experience of using customer feedback and performance data to drive service improvements and enhance the customer experience. Experience of working with leisure management systems and supporting systems development, implementation, or optimisation. Experience of planning and facilitating workshops, focus groups, training, or stakeholder engagement activities. Strong stakeholder management skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate, and promote best practice. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Sport, Exercise & Health This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 22/10/2026 For informal queries please contact Jo Blackett 07989 158548 jo.blackett@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391221": {
      "title": "EPSRC Postdoctoral Pathway Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108703 The role: The EPSRC Postdoctoral Pathway supports EPSRC-funded PhD graduates who are excited about taking the first steps towards research independence with a two-year Fellowship at the University of Bristol. The scheme offers the opportunity to launch your own, independent research career in an exciting and innovative research environment. Postdoctoral Pathway Fellows will propose their own bespoke programme to conduct innovative research at the frontiers of Science and Engineering. Project proposals are invited across the full range of topics in the EPSRC research portfolio EPSRC strategy, remit and programmes – UKRI . Successful applicants will have the chance to take their research to the next level, whether by delivering high-impact original research, or by exploiting new interdisciplinary research opportunities. The Postdoctoral Pathway award should not be viewed as an opportunity to complete PhD work. What will you be doing?: Successful applicants will receive funding for up to 2 years. Benefits include: Work within world-class research groups at the University of Bristol In addition to a Project Sponsor (a Bristol academic staff member supporting your project proposal), you will have access to an academic mentor for advice on research and career development Access to personal and professional development opportunities. Postdoctoral Pathway Fellows will be recruited at Grade J on the University of Bristol salary scale and receive a grant of £5,000 for research costs. You should apply if: This scheme is designed to support Early Career Researchers who are nearing completion of their PhD or have recently completed it. Eligibility criteria: All applicants must have received PhD studentship support from EPSRC (stipend and/or fees) or training award, for example CDT, ICASE, or IDLA studentship Applicants must not hold a permanent academic position Applicants must have submitted their PhD thesis before the post begins and meet either of the following two requirements: Applicants who have recently completed their PhD they must have had their viva on or after 1st November 2025 (12 months prior to the application deadline) Applicants who are near completion of their PhD must intend to submit their doctoral thesis on or before 31st January 2027 (within 3 months of the application deadline). If they do not submit their thesis by this date, the award offer will be withdrawn. Applicants who are yet to submit must include a statement of support from their PhD supervisor confirming their expected submission timeline. Additional information: Whilst this vacancy is administratively linked to the School of Engineering Mathematics & Technology (SEMT), applications are welcomed from eligible candidates across all Schools To apply for this role please download and complete the attached documents (as specified in the Application Guidance). Alongside completing the other sections of the application, the additional documents should be uploaded to the ‘Other documents' section of the application portal. Please see the Application Guidance for more details. Please contact doctoral-prize@bristol.ac.uk with any queries. Contract type: Fixed term up to 24 months Work pattern: Full time Grade: J/Pathway 2 Salary: £44,746 - £50253 per annum plus a a grant of £5,000 for research costs School/Unit: School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 The interview date is anticipated to take place on Thursday 10th December 2026 For informal queries please contact: Laura Maclean, Senior Research Administrator Email: EPSRC-Postdoctoral-Pathway@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391500": {
      "title": "Faculty Head of Technical Services (FSE)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113758 The role: Shape the technical services that enable world-leading science and engineering This is an opportunity to lead a large and diverse technical community, influence Faculty strategy and ensure that outstanding people, facilities and services enable excellent research, education, innovation and external partnership. As Faculty Head of Technical Services, you will provide visible, strategic leadership for all technical services across the Faculty of Science and Engineering. Reporting to the Director of Faculty Operations and working as part of the Faculty Leadership Team, you will shape a complex service spanning diverse scientific and engineering disciplines, teams, specialist facilities, infrastructure, budgets and risks. You will ensure that technical services are safe, resilient, efficient and ready for the future. More than maintaining excellent operations, you will bring technical insight into senior decision-making, influence research and education priorities, create opportunities for collaboration and innovation, and help the Faculty make the best use of its people, facilities and resources. The role offers substantial scope to lead change. You will develop technical capability and culture, guide workforce and infrastructure planning, build strong partnerships across schools and professional services, and represent the Faculty and University internally and externally. You will also contribute to University-wide technical leadership and may deputise for the Director of Faculty Operations when required. Who will thrive here? A credible and inclusive senior leader who can see the whole system: people, safety, service quality, infrastructure, finance, research, education and long-term capability. You will be comfortable making difficult priorities clear, influencing at senior level and leading through complexity without losing sight of staff and student experience. What will you be doing?: Lead people, culture and change: Provide visible leadership to a large, multidisciplinary technical community. Build a culture of accountability, inclusion, innovation, collaboration, performance and continuous improvement while leading significant change and strategic projects. Shape technical services for the future: Design, evaluate and continuously improve a complex service model that meets current and future research, education and operational needs, including workforce planning, resilience and service quality. Direct facilities, infrastructure and investment: Set priorities for technical facilities, infrastructure projects, specialist equipment and purchases. Develop strong business cases and ensure resources are used effectively. Assure safety, risk and continuity: Lead operational health and safety, regulatory compliance, risk management and business continuity, creating clear ownership and confidence across the Faculty. Influence Faculty and University strategy: Bring authoritative technical insight to senior decision-making, contribute to the Faculty Leadership Team and University-wide initiatives, and represent Bristol with internal and external partners. Build partnerships and unlock value: Develop strong relationships with academic and professional-services leaders. Create collaboration across schools and faculties, support innovation and identify commercial, grant-funded and revenue opportunities. You should apply if: Strategic leadership and change: Significant senior leadership and management experience, including visible leadership of highly diverse technical teams and delivery of complex organisational change. Technical credibility: Highly developed expertise in a relevant technical discipline, together with sufficient breadth to understand different technical environments and turn evidence into effective strategic solutions. Safety, risk and resilience: Substantial experience of operational safety, business continuity, regulatory compliance, risk management and constructive conflict resolution. Finance, planning and improvement: Experience of planning and controlling substantial budgets, prioritising competing demands, building business cases and delivering measurable continuous improvement. Influence and inclusive people leadership: The ability to direct, negotiate and influence across a complex stakeholder environment, and to engage, motivate and inspire teams with diverse perspectives and priorities. Professional standing: Knowledge gained through a relevant degree, professional qualification or registration, supported by significant professional experience, plus management or leadership training. Professional registration and a NEBOSH Certificate are desirable. We are interested in evidence of impact and transferable leadership. Your experience may have been gained in higher education, a research institute, a national laboratory, life sciences, advanced manufacturing, engineering or another complex technical environment. For a full view of the requirements and responsbilities, please refer to the job description here . Additional information: Why Bristol The University of Bristol exists to make a positive impact locally, nationally and globally through distinctive education, innovative research, excellence, inclusivity and partnership. Its 2030 vision is to be firmly established among the world’s top 50 great research-intensive universities and the UK top 10. The Faculty of Science and Engineering’s 2025-2030 strategy brings together nine schools around a shared mission: developing and applying world-leading scientific knowledge, understanding and technology to address global challenges and improve lives. The Faculty is prioritising interdisciplinary research, innovative education, strong partnerships and investment in its most promising areas. This is a particularly exciting time to join. The Faculty’s strengths include advanced computation and quantum technology, AI and data science, digital chemistry, energy and environment, health and living, materials and manufacturing, networks and security, and novel materials. Major infrastructure and innovation include the University’s investment connected to the Isambard-AI supercomputer and the new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus, which is opening in 2026 as a collaborative hub for research, education, enterprise and civic partnership. Bristol also offers a strong employment proposition. University benefits include pension provision, learning and development, wellbeing support, staff networks, sustainable travel benefits and relocation support for eligible staff. Work-life balance provisions include a typical 35-hour week, flexible-working options, 25 days of annual leave, seven University closure days and eight bank holidays, subject to the relevant policies and the requirements of the role. Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Grade: M Salary: £77,537 - £91,082 School/Unit: The Faculty of Science and Engineering This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday, 18th October. For informal queries please contact: Dani Murtagh, Director of Faculty Operations (Faculty of Science and Engineering) - dani.murtagh@bristol.ac.uk. The anticipated date for the selection process is Thursday, 5th November. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391715": {
      "title": "Health and Fitness Adviser",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Centre for Sport Exercise and Health Reference: SUPP113844 The role: You will play a key role in supporting our Health and Fitness facilities, helping customers lead happier and healthier lives. Responsibilities include supervising the gym, free weights area, and exercise studios to ensure all users can exercise in a safe, clean, welcoming, and enjoyable environment. The role also involves carrying out administrative tasks, maintaining accurate membership records, and ensuring all relevant documentation is up to date. You will be encouraged to develop your knowledge of health, fitness, and exercise through continuous self-directed learning. As part of a varied and flexible work programme, you will deliver support across a range of services, including exercise classes, Health Minds sessions, personal training activities, and gym floor supervision within our Health & Fitness facilities. Flexibility and a customer-focused approach are essential to successfully meet the needs of our members and visitors. What will you be doing?: You will undertake a varied role supporting customers across our Health & Fitness facilities. Responsibilities will include gym floor supervision, maintaining high standards of cleanliness, delivering fitness classes, providing personal training support, and assisting within specialist programmes, including our Healthy Minds service. You should apply if: You have: Experience working in a fitness environment Enthusiasm to help members Recognised fitness qualification Good communication skills Ability to deliver group fitness classes Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: 20 hours per week Grade: E Salary: £26,093 - £28,778 per annum School/Unit: Centre for Sport, Exercise and Health This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 26th October 2026 For informal queries please contact James.Rowntree@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391559": {
      "title": "Heilbronn Research Fellowships in Pure Mathematics, Data Science, and Quantum Computing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108722 The role: The Heilbronn Institute for Mathematical Research invites applications for Heilbronn Research Fellowships. Fellows divide their time equally between their own research and the classified research programme of the Heilbronn Institute. Research areas of interest include, but are not restricted to, Algebra, Algebraic Geometry, Combinatorics, Data Science, Number Theory, Probability, and Quantum Computing. Fellows have previously been appointed with backgrounds in most areas of Pure Mathematics and Statistics, and Mathematical/Theoretical Physics. We expect to make up to 10 appointments at the University of Bristol , Imperial College London , King’s College London , UCL , and the University of Manchester . These appointments will mainly be in Pure Mathematics with up to two of the appointments at the University of Bristol in Data Science or Quantum Computing. The salary range is £46,874 - £52,517 (or local equivalent) depending on previous experience, plus the London weighting where appropriate. There is a salary supplement of £3.5K pa, in recognition of the distinctive nature of these Fellowships. Payment of this supplement is conditional on a PhD thesis having been accepted in final form. The positions are 3 years with fixed-term funding until 2030 with a preferred start date of 1 October 2027. Extensions of up to three years may be available, which can be held at a wider range of universities in the UK. What will you be doing?: Fellows divide their time equally between their own research and the classified research programme of the Heilbronn Institute, thus enjoying opportunities to engage in collaborative work as well as individual projects. Fellows join a cohort of around 50 Heilbronn Research Fellows that includes some of the leading early-career mathematicians in the UK. Expert mentoring will be provided by the host department, with a national programme of career development events overseen by our Associate Chairs, who are based in the School of Mathematics at the University of Bristol, Imperial College, and the University of Manchester. The internal research programme is determined by the Head of HIMR in conjunction with GCHQ. Fellows are presented with a range of challenging mathematical problems and are given the facilities and support to contribute to one or more of them, frequently as part of a collaborative team. You should apply if: Due to the nature of the Institute's work, Fellows must apply for a national security clearance at an appropriate level before appointment. Applications are open only to UK residents who are UK, or dual UK nationals : applicants are welcome to contact the Heilbronn Manager at himr-recruitment@bristol.ac.uk to discuss eligibility before applying. The Heilbronn Institute is a supporter of the LMS Good Practice Scheme aimed at advancing women’s careers in mathematics and we particularly welcome applications from women. Additional information: Applications will be made through mathjobs.org Applicants will need to submit a cover letter, indicating the institutions where they prefer to hold their Fellowship from the above list of partner universities. In addition, we require a CV and a one-page statement of external research objectives over the 3 year Fellowship term. Applicants must arrange for three letters of reference to be uploaded to the mathjobs.org website, prior to the closing date. The application deadline is 11.59pm GMT, Sunday 8 th November 2026. We anticipate holding interviews over the 11 th - 14th of January 2027. For informal queries please contact: himr-recruitment@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391689": {
      "title": "HR Shared Services Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Human Resources Reference: SUPP113801 The role: This is an exciting opportunity for you to make a real difference within the Employee Services Hub to play a pivotal role in the HR shared services administrative operation, delivering a confidential, efficient, and effective professional HR and administrative support service. This role will allow you to explore the opportunities that a career in HR can deliver. What will you be doing?: You will provide key support as first point of contact for a range of day-to-day queries from internal and external customers. You will be responsible for end-to-end administration across employment lifecycle activity, focusing on onboarding and employee life cycle tasks, providing advice and guidance on HR processes and procedures. You should apply if: You have proven experience in a high volume, busy administrative or HR transactional role, as well as excellent Office 365 skills, a quick learner with the ability to pick up new systems and processes with ease You will be a natural problem solver, always looking for the answer yourself before asking how to solve an issue You have excellent organisational, time management and administrative skills, including the ability to manage and prioritise a busy workload to meet deadlines You have excellent interpersonal skills, a positive customer-focused approach and are a great team player You enjoy working in a busy environment and can respond positively to change Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term Contract - Maternity cover for 12 months Work pattern: Full time - 35 hours Grade: F Salary: £28,778 - £32,080 per annum School/Unit: Human Resources - Employee Services Hub This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Sunday 25th October For informal queries please contact: Sharon Faulkner (Shared Services Team Leader) on Sharon.Faulkner@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391676": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108476 The role: The School of Computer Science at the University of Bristol is seeking to grow our thriving and talented AI community by hiring a new AI lecturer. We have a long-standing track record for world-leading AI, and recent successes place us at the forefront of UK AI research and teaching. Bristol hosts Isambard AI, the UK’s national AI supercomputing facility, leads two of the UK’s nine UKRI National AI Research Hubs (in Collective Intelligence and Information Theory), runs back-to-back UKRI Centres for Doctoral Training in Interactive AI and Practice-Oriented AI, and has recently launched an innovative undergraduate degree in Computer Science with AI. Bristol’s world-class AI research spans applied and theoretical work in intelligent agents, machine learning, computer vision, reasoning, and reinforcement learning, and tackles problems in multimodal and interactive AI. We solve interdisciplinary challenges in collaboration with researchers in creative technologies, the environmental and life sciences, and robotics. We encourage applicants from any area of AI to apply and are specifically interested in candidates with strengths in Multi-Modal AI and Computer Vision, and those that bring new kinds of AI expertise to the School. The School values diversity and is committed to creating an inclusive environment where all staff can thrive. We particularly welcome applications from individuals who wish to work part-time, to job-share, or who require flexible working patterns. We are happy to discuss these possibilities with candidates at any stage of the recruitment process.‑time, job‑share, or who require flexible working patterns. We are happy to discuss these possibilities with candidates at any stage of the recruitment process What will you be doing?: The postholder will contribute to the University’s impressive record of world-leading research (as demonstrated in REF 2021 which ranked the School 7th in the UK for CS research) and to developing and delivering the School’s education programmes (including our new undergraduate degree in Computer Science with AI). The School prides itself on its academic quality and its collegiality. We are incredibly proud of our outstanding working environment with a dedicated professional services team, a very high standard of achievement in research and education, and a culture of inclusivity and kindness. You should apply if: Candidates will have a PhD in Computer Science or a closely related discipline (or equivalent industrial experience) and a track record of publishing in top academic venues and applying for external funding appropriate to their career stage. The potential for leadership, undertaking research collaborations with colleagues from top international labs, and collaborating on impactful translation of ideas with relevant industrial partners is desirable. The successful candidate will be able to design and deliver excellent teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate level and will be committed to making the most of growing opportunities in this area. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open Ended Work pattern: Full-time Grade: J or K Salary: Grade J £43,482 - £53,253 per annum or Grade K £50,253 - £58,225 per annum School/Unit: The School of Computer Science Advert/selection timeline: This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Friday 6th November 2026 Shortlisting: Tuesday 24 th November Interviews: Presentations Thursday 3 rd December and interview panel Friday 4 th December For informal queries please contact: Dima Damen, dima.damen@bristol.ac.uk and Seth Bullock, seth.bullock@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391455": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Pure Mathematics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108709 The role: The School of Mathematics is seeking to appoint a Lecturer (analogous to Assistant Professor) or Senior Lecturer in Pure Mathematics, with the level of appointment depending on the experience of the successful candidate. We are looking for a world-class researcher in Logic or Set Theory. We interpret these areas broadly, and although preference would be given to a candidate in set theory, it is not limited to such, and candidates in other areas of modern logic, such as computability theory, model theory and proof theory, are encouraged to apply. What will you be doing?: The post-holder will be affiliated with the School’s Institute for Pure Mathematics and will be responsible for their own research programme, undertaking independent research. The post-holder will also teach undergraduate and postgraduate students and take on appropriate administrative duties. They will be a key member of the Logic and Set Theory Group at Bristol, which is an internationally leading centre for research in set theory and the philosophy and foundations of mathematics. You should apply if: You have a passion for developing and leading innovative and impactful research in Logic and Set Theory. You have demonstrated the self-motivation, initiative, and organisational skills required to carry out a successful research programme. You have a proven publication record in internationally recognised, peer-reviewed scientific journals. Additional information: References: It is the applicant's responsibility to ensure that three reference letters are sent to maths-recruitment@bristol.ac.uk by 23:59 on Sunday 1st November 2026 . Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full time/100% time Grade: J, K or L Salary: Grade J in the range £44,746 – £50,253 or Grade K in the range £51,753 – £58,225 or Grade L in the £58,225 – £69,488, per annum (appointed at grade depending on experience) School/Unit: School of Mathematics This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 It is expected that the final selection process will be held in November 2026 For informal queries please contact one of the following contacts: Prof Oliver Johnson, Head of School, headofschool-maths@bristol.ac.uk Prof Tim Burness, Professor of Pure Mathematics Director of the Institute for Pure Mathematics, t.burness@bristol.ac.uk Prof Philip Welch, Professor of Pure Mathematics p.welch@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391584": {
      "title": "Philanthropy Officer (Fixed-term contract)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Global Engagement Reference: SUPP113660 The role: Philanthropic support creates meaningful opportunities for students and researchers at the University of Bristol. The Philanthropy Officer will work in the Leadership Giving team, responsible for cultivating strong relationships with existing and potential donors to raise funds for University priorities in the £1,000 - £10,000 range, by contacting Bristol alumni and friends with tailored communications (post, phone and digital) and creating inspirational updates and fundraising asks with the help of colleagues. This role can be considered on a part-time or full-time basis, between 0.6 and 1.0 FTE. Applicants should state their preferred FTE arrangement in their cover letter. Part-time hours will be calculated on a pro rata basis (for example, 0.6 FTE equates to 21 hours per week). Please note that this role is a fixed-term contract ending on 12th October 2027. What will you be doing?: You will help with the operational delivery of effective fundraising and stewardship to inspire donations at Leadership Giving level (£1,000-£10,000). This includes: Creating personalised fundraising and engagement communications to inspire our Leadership Giving donors and potential donors Building relationships with individual supporters through tailored emails and conversations Working closely with colleagues in our Regular Giving and Individual Philanthropy teams to support to secure donations and steward donors effectively Collaboration with colleagues across our engagement, events, data, insight, marketing and communications teams Project management of our US Leadership Giving activity Evaluating and analysing Leadership Giving fundraising activity results You should apply if: You have experience working in fundraising or donor relations and have the confidence to make direct philanthropic asks You have excellent organisation skills, you can juggle multiple projects and deadlines, and have a strong attention to detail. You can communicate excellently in writing and verbally. You have the ability to build positive working relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, both internal and external You are passionate about supporting Bristol students from low-income backgrounds, enabling ground-breaking research and have a belief in the crucial role universities play in society. For a full view of the responsibilities and requirements, please refer to the attached job description. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed-term contract until 12/10/2027. If you are an internal applicant and currently hold a substantive post and would like to consider this opportunity on a secondment basis, this is welcomed. Please ensure you have discussed this with your current line manager before applying. Work pattern: Monday - Friday, 35 hours per week. Though the role can be considered on a part-time basis (minimum 0.6 FTE). Hybrid working arrangement: Team day working on campus is a Wednesday each week, however we are often in the office more often due to events taking place on various days of the week. Grade: H Salary: £35,608 - £39,906 per annum (pro-rated for part time) School/Unit: Global Engagement Division (GED) For informal queries please contact: Alicia Jago - Deputy Director (Fundraising and Stewardship) - Alicia.Jago@bristol.ac.uk. The anticipated interview date is the 2nd November This advert will close on Sunday, 18th October at 23.59pm UK time. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=390478": {
      "title": "Professor of Cyber Security",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108475 The role: The University of Bristol is seeking to appoint an outstanding Professor of Cyber Security to join the internationally recognised Bristol Cyber Security Group (BCSG) in the School of Computer Science. This is an exceptional opportunity for an established academic leader to shape the future of cyber security research and education within one of the UK's leading research-intensive universities. We are looking for an internationally recognised scholar whose research aligns with and complements the strengths of the BCSG and who can provide strategic leadership, inspire colleagues and students, and deliver world-leading research with real-world impact. BCSG is one of the UK's largest and most influential cyber security research communities, bringing together researchers across cyber-physical systems, software security, usable security and privacy, and cybercrime and online harms. The Group has a strong track record of securing major research funding, influencing policy and practice, and delivering impactful interdisciplinary research in collaboration with industry, government and international partners. What will you be doing?: As Professor of Cyber Security, you will: Lead and deliver internationally recognised research in an area aligned with the BCSG. Develop an ambitious research vision and secure significant external funding. Build and lead high-performing research teams and collaborative partnerships. Contribute to the strategic direction and growth of cyber security research within the School and University. Deliver innovative teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. Supervise and mentor PhD students, postdoctoral researchers and academic colleagues. Play a leading role in fostering an inclusive, collaborative and intellectually vibrant academic community. You should apply if: You can demonstrate: An international reputation for research excellence in cyber security, in an area aligned with BCSG. A track record of publication in leading conferences and peer-reviewed journals, or equivalent industrial research leadership. A sustained record of securing external research funding and leading significant research programmes. Experience of teaching and supervising undergraduate and postgraduate students. A proven ability to lead, develop and grow a research area, team or community. A commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration and delivering research with academic, societal and industrial impact. For a full view of the requirements and responsibilities, please refer to the attached candidate brochure. Why Bristol? This is an exciting time to join the University of Bristol. As a member of the Russell Group, Bristol is recognised globally for the quality and impact of its research and education. The University is ranked among the world's leading universities and is committed to ambitious growth in research, innovation and global engagement. You will join a School of Computer Science that is expanding significantly and forms a key part of the University's new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus, a landmark development designed to bring together researchers, students, industry and public sector partners to tackle some of society's biggest challenges. BCSG is a flagship tenant in Temple Quarter, occupying its new bespoke facility in August 2026, complete with CNI lab, dedicated IoT lab and secure data centre. BCSG offers a uniquely supportive and collaborative environment, with over 80 researchers working across multiple disciplines and a strong portfolio of major research programmes, doctoral training initiatives and industry partnerships. Researchers benefit from extensive connections with government, the National Cyber Security Centre, UKRI, international collaborators and leading organisations across the technology sector. You'll also be part of a university driving innovation at scale, with access to world-class facilities and opportunities for collaboration across areas including AI, data science, digital futures, engineering, social sciences and policy. Bristol is home to the UK's flagship AI supercomputing infrastructure, helping to position the University at the forefront of digital and technological research. Located in one of the UK's most vibrant and creative cities, Bristol offers an outstanding quality of life, a thriving technology ecosystem, excellent transport links and a strong culture of innovation and entrepreneurship. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: M - Professorial Range 1 Salary: £74,828 - £77,537 per annum School/Unit: Computer Science This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 Shortlisting will take place week commencing the 2nd November 2026 Interviews are anticipated to take place week commencing the 16th or 23rd November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Mike Fraser, Mike.Fraser@bristol.ac.uk Inah Omoronyia, inah.omoronyia@bristol.ac.uk Awais Rashid, awais.rashid@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391650": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108724 The role: We have an exciting opportunity for a talented systematic reviewer or a quantitative researcher with an interest in evidence synthesis to join the BEAM (Bristol Evidence Synthesis, Appraisal and Modelling) Centre at the University of Bristol. BEAM brings together researchers working on evidence synthesis and modelling research across Bristol Medical School. BEAM is an internationally renowned centre of excellence in evidence synthesis and is the only centre to host all three types of NIHR funded Evidence Synthesis Groups (ESGs), providing access to a wealth of knowledge and expertise in evidence synthesis across a broad range of topics and clinical areas. You will work primarily within the NIHR Bristol ESG and NIHR BUcKLES ESG but with some flexibility to contribute to the work of other research groups within BEAM where needed. The Bristol ESG produces evidence reviews on a wide range of topics for the NIHR. The BUcKLES group is the only ESG dedicated to undertaking living systematic reviews. You will be part of a multidisciplinary team including systematic reviewers, statisticians, information specialists, qualitative researchers, and health economists. You will also work closely with clinical experts and public contributors. The post is available full time from the 1 st November 2026 until 31/03/2030, but part-time working will be considered at a minimum of 0.6 FTE. Requests for flexible working patterns will be considered. Hybrid working is available: we encourage staff to work in the office as face-to-face working and regular contact with colleagues are key to a healthy workplace. The postholder would be expected to attend the office a minimum of 1 to 2 days in a normal working week with no restriction on which days these are. What will you be doing?: Projects will include applied evidence syntheses, from evidence maps and rapid reviews to full systematic reviews, and may include methodological work related to evidence synthesis. You will be required to contribute to: The day-to-day review processes and research-related administration of conducting systematic reviews and other evidence syntheses. Developing review protocols, screening studies for inclusion, data collection, risk of bias and GRADE assessments, preparation of datasets, interpretation of data and writing reports. Narrative and quantitative syntheses (e.g. meta-analyses) for a wide range of projects. Peer reviewed publications, grant proposals and conference presentations You should apply if: You have strong training in quantitative research, including knowledge of basic concepts in medical statistics You have an enthusiasm for evidence synthesis and to contribute to living systematic reviews. You have some experience of working on systematic reviews, although this is not a requirement and training will be available You have excellent critical appraisal skills for a range of studies designs For SRA: you have a relevant PhD. For RA: you have a relevant Masters degree (e.g. epidemiology, public health, medical statistics or other relevant quantitative discipline). Additional information: Contract type: Open ended with funding until 31/03/2030 (start date to be agreed, but likely to be 01/11/2026) Work pattern: Full time/ 1 FTE (part-time working will be considered at minimum 0.6 FTE) Grade: I or J/ Pathway 2 Salary: I: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum, J: £44,746 - £50,253 per annum (pro rata if part time) School/Unit: Bristol Medical School Shift pattern: 35 hours per week (part time considered) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 29/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Deborah Caldwell ( Professor of Epidemiology and Public Health: d.m.caldwell@bristol.ac.uk and Julian Higgins ( Professor of Evidence Synthesis, : julian.higgins@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391366": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Animal Behaviour",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108721 The role: We are seeking to appoint a Research Associate to support a BBSRC grant funded under its spotlight call in the area of ‘Fundamental research to enhance animal welfare' and specifically 'measures of welfare, including developing and validating new measures'. Funding is available for 12 months in the first instance with the expectation this will be extended for a further 12 months. The project seeks to develop methods that enable the objective quantification of affective state in different species based on reward learning. We have shown impairments in reward learning have the potential to provide a trans-species, behavioural biomarker of affective state. This project will develop automated methods to quantify reward learning in chickens and dogs as well as expanding our studies in mice. The project will include in lab and field-based behavioural studies and working with engineers to develop novel automated devices. The project includes plans to develop species relevant devices which can be integrated with RFID detection to enable individualised programming and data collection. The ultimate goal will be to validate both the concept of reward learning as a behavioural biomarker of affective state and methods to enable its quantification to facilitate unbiased assessment of animal welfare in different species. What will you be doing?: You will join a team of researchers working across neuroscience, psychopharmacology and animal welfare. The main purpose of the role will be to work with the project engineers and specialist technician to develop and test novel reward delivery devices in different species. This will include input to the design of the devices, evaluating prototypes in mice, chickens and dogs and developing optimised training and testing protocols. We will then run proof of concept studies in the field-testing sensitivity to different affective state. There will also be an opportunity to develop coding expertise both for the development of the behavioural testing protocols and integration with RFID chip readers. You should apply if: You have a passion for animal behaviour and interest in developing novel behavioural technologies. Previous experience in either animal welfare and behaviour, neuroscience and psychology, and/or engineering and computer science would be beneficial, but we recognise this project will involve a range of skills and there will be opportunities to gain additional training where needed. You will need to be comfortable working with a different species and visiting local farms or people’s homes. Good organisational skills are essential as well as an ability to manage your time and multitask. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term (funding available until 30/11/2027) Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: School of Psychology and Neuroscience This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 21st October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Emma Robinson, Professor of Psychopharmacology via email: emma.s.j.robinson@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=341894": {
      "title": "Research Associate in QET LABS /Senior Research Associate",
      "description": "Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD1GA00002 The role: We have a number of exciting research opportunities in the Quantum Engineering Technology Labs ( QET Labs ) at the University of Bristol. As a member of QET Labs, you will conduct research at the interface of quantum technologies, photonics, and experimental science. You will be part of a dynamic team and have the opportunity to interact with all members of QET Labs and the wider Bristol Quantum Information Institute. QET Labs has a mission to take quantum science discoveries out of the lab and engineer them into technologies for the benefit of society. This includes novel routes to quantum computing hardware, quantum communications, enhanced sensing & imaging and new platforms to investigate fundamental quantum physics. The Research Centre consists of over 100 academics, researchers, staff and students and sits within both the School of Physics and the School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering. Multiple Research Associate positions are available of different durations. The duration of the post to be offered will be discussed following a successful interview. What will you be doing?: Your responsibilities will include working with others to formulate project plans, developing new techniques for classical and quantum optics characterisation and delivery of research outputs. Specifically, you will be working in one of these key areas of research: Quantum Computing Quantum Communications Quantum Sensing/Imaging Solid State quantum systems Integrated Quantum Photonics Pure or applied quantum information theory Cold and ultra cold atomic Physics You should apply if: You have a strong track record in the design, implementation, and analysis of photonic devices or quantum information processing with photons You have expertise in optical engineering or quantum optics and a demonstratable understanding of its major platforms, e.g. integrated photonics, emitters You have postgraduate (or equivalent) laboratory experience in one of experimental physics, optics, photonics, electronics, or a related discipline You have a PhD (or equivalent qualification) and first degree in Physics, Electrical/ Electronic Engineering, or other relevant discipline (or equivalent relevant research experience) This position is presented as full-time. Flexible working options are considered. QET Labs is committed to helping researchers become independent academics and have extensive experience in aiding PDRAs with fellowship applications. Please note this is NOT a direct application for a live vacancy. Instead, you will be entered into a candidate pool for a RESEARCH ASSOCIATE IN QET LABS /SENIOR RESEARCH ASSOCIATE position. We have a policy in place to regularly review our candidate pool to ensure its relevance and efficiency therefore you may be invited to interview or occasionally receive notifications regarding new job openings. We encourage you to stay connected with us and to keep your contact information up to date to ensure that you don't miss out on any potential opportunities. If you have any questions or need further assistance, please don't hesitate to reach out to us at recruitment@bristol.ac.uk . Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391721": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Senior Research Associate in Climate Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108738 The role: We are pleased to announce the exciting opportunity of an 18-month Postdoctoral Research Assistant position (Senior Research Associate) at the University of Bristol, UK, in collaboration with the University of Colorado at Boulder, USA, as part of the NSFGEO-NERC funded project \"How well can we constrain past changes in ocean heat and freshwater content?\". The successful candidate will join a US-UK team funded by NSF and NERC, led by the University of Colorado at Boulder, USA (Dr Donata Giglio). The work at the University of Bristol is led by Prof Matt Palmer who also holds the position of Science Fellow at the Met Office Hadley Centre. The research will focus on in situ observations of ocean temperature and the development of ensemble datasets that fully account the uncertainty associated with inter-platform biases. This work will build on research performed at the Met Office Hadley Centre and involve regular contact with scientists working in the Climate Monitoring & Attribution team. The new ensemble datasets will be combined with novel mapping approaches developed by US colleagues at the University of Colorado to provide 3D time-evolving estimates of historical heat and freshwater content. The job holder will also work with scientists at the International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis and the Met Office Hadley Centre to develop and analyse new surface temperature and sea-level rise projections as part of the project. There will be opportunities to visit the US Team in Colorado and attend international conferences and/or working group meetings to present results as part of the project. More details of the project are available from Matt Palmer on request ( m.d.palmer@bristol.ac.uk ). What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will be responsible for developing a new ensemble ocean temperature profile dataset that fully characterises the uncertainty associated with historical inter-platform biases (i.e. differences in apparent ocean temperature that arise through sensor/device characteristics). They will work alongside the US-led team to develop these into ensemble estimates of ocean heat uptake. This improved knowledge of ocean heat uptake and its uncertainty will be used to develop a new set of surface temperature and sea-level rise projections in collaboration with scientists at the Met Office Hadley Centre (UK) and International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis (Austria). In addition to the functional aspects of the work, there will be opportunities for research collaboration across many aspects of the project, including mechanistic analysis of ocean heat and freshwater content change and the impact of the new observational constraints on projections of global climate change. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to visit the US-based team at the University of Colorado at Boulder. You should apply if: You will have a PhD or equivalent professional experience in a relevant science discipline. You should also have: Proficiency in the Python programming language Strong quantitative and data analysis skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively And ideally: Experience of modern software development practices, including version control and use of online repositories, such as GitHub Experience in the analysis of geophysical datasets Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding from 01/02/2027 – 31/07/2028 Work pattern: 1 FTE/35 hours per week Grade: I/J Salary: Grade I £33,906 – £44,746 per annum OR Grade J £44,746 - £50,253 per annum School/Unit: Earth Sciences Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 16th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Matt Palmer, m.d.palmer@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391718": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Senior Research Associate in genome-scale metabolic modelling for red blood cell engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108715 The role: Join the School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology at the University of Bristol as a Postdoctoral Research Associate in genome-scale metabolic modelling for red blood cell engineering. This exciting research position forms part of the highly collaborative Aerobic Engineering Biology programme, led by Professor Ashley Toye, which brings together researchers from the University of Bristol, Northwestern University and industrial partners to engineer red blood cells capable of sensing and responding to changes in their metabolic environment. You will lead the metabolic modelling aspects of this programme, developing and applying genome-scale mathematical models to understand red blood cell maturation and predict how engineered biological circuits interact with endogenous cellular metabolism. Working in Professor Lucia Marucci’s group and alongside experimental researchers, you will help create an iterative modelling–experiment cycle that informs the design and optimisation of engineered therapeutic red blood cells. What will you be doing?: You will: Develop and refine genome-scale metabolic models of reticulocytes and mature red blood cells using published knowledge and experimental data generated through the project. Build mathematical models of engineered gene and enzyme circuits and integrate these into broader metabolic modelling frameworks. Analyse metabolomics and other experimental datasets to investigate metabolic changes during red blood cell maturation and engineering. Collaborate closely with experimental researchers at the University of Bristol and Northwestern University to validate models and optimise synthetic circuit design. Work with academic collaborators, industrial partners and the wider research consortium to advance project objectives. Maintain accurate records of code, computational models and research outputs to support reproducible research. Contribute to monthly project progress reports and dissemination of research findings through high-quality publications. You should apply if: You are an enthusiastic researcher with expertise in genome-scale metabolic modelling, mathematical modelling and programming, and you are excited by interdisciplinary research at the interface of engineering biology and computational modelling. You should have: A PhD (or be close to completion) or equivalent research experience in mathematics, bioengineering, engineering biology, bioinformatics, biotechnology or a closely related discipline. Expertise in genome-scale metabolic modelling. Strong mathematical modelling and programming skills. Experience working collaboratively with experimental scientists. Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to publish high-quality research. The ability to work independently while contributing effectively within a multidisciplinary team and delivering research to agreed deadlines. Experience in engineering biology, multiomics data integration, large collaborative research projects or research funding applications would be advantageous. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding until 05/12/2027 Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I or J Salary: £39,906-£44,746 OR £43,482-£50,253 per annum (depending on experience) School/Unit: School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 18th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Lucia Marucci lucia.marucci@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391684": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Industrial Computer Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108487 The role: The School of Computer Science at the University of Bristol seeks to appoint a Senior Lecturer in Industrial Computer Science to act as academic industry lead within the School. This is a strategic leadership role focused on delivering cutting edge industry education and project skills to our talented students, strengthening our engagement with industrial partners, enhancing graduate employability, developing student and staff entrepreneurship, and aligning education and research with rapidly evolving cutting edge computer science innovations. Working closely with the Head of School and academic and professional colleagues across education, research and impact, you will shape and drive education and partnerships spanning the full range of CS disciplines, formulating and driving forward the School’s industrial strategy. What will you be doing?: Working with professional services colleagues in the School and Faculty, you will deliver pivotal teaching that brings together industry partners and student projects; establish and support the School’s Industrial Advisory Board; lead placement, internship and year-in-industry opportunities; and influence our education to align the curriculum with industry-leading tools and practices. You will promote AI literacy, professional skills development, and international employability, championing student entrepreneurship, staff spinouts and research impact. The role requires coordination within the beyond the School to consolidate employer relationships and enhance students’ transition into a competitive graduate market. You will also have the opportunity to explore entrepreneurial ambitions for yourself and others as part of the School’s participation in the new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus. You should apply if: You have a strong track record in cutting-edge computer science or related fields, alongside substantial experience of industry collaboration, knowledge exchange or entrepreneurship. You will bring credibility with employers, experience of building strategic partnerships, and an understanding of labour market trends in computing. Leadership experience, excellent education and communication skills, and your commitment to student employability and innovation are both important. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: L Salary: £58,225 - £69,488 per annum School/Unit: Computer Science School Team This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 The interviews are anticipated to take place on Friday 20th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Mike Fraser, mike.fraser@bristol.ac.uk , Head of Computer Science Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values. The University of Bristol aims to be a place where everyone feels able to be themselves and do their best in an inclusive working environment where all colleagues can thrive and reach their full potential. We want to attract, develop, and retain individuals with different experiences, backgrounds and perspectives – particularly people of colour, LGBT+ and disabled people - because diversity of people and ideas remains integral to our excellence as a global civic institution."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391618": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Laser Physics for Lithium Isotope Enrichment",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108731 The role: The University of Bristol is seeking a Senior Research Associate to join an ambitious, industrially funded programme focused on the development of advanced laser-based technologies for lithium isotope enrichment. Based within the Interface Analysis Centre, School of Physics, the role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a strategically important research programme that brings together laser physics, spectroscopy, vacuum engineering and process development. Working within a multidisciplinary team led by Professor Tom Scott, Dr Jacek Wasik and Dr Dave Megson-Smith, you will provide specialist expertise in laser physics and optical systems while helping to establish a new experimental capability. The position offers a unique opportunity to work at the interface between academic research and industrial innovation, contributing to the development of novel technologies with the potential for significant scientific and commercial impact. What will you be doing?: You will lead the development, commissioning and optimisation of advanced laser and optical systems that underpin the isotope enrichment process. This will include designing and integrating laser sources, spectroscopy tools and optical diagnostics, while ensuring the performance, stability and precision required for experimental success. A major focus of the role will be planning and conducting spectroscopy and process-development experiments to investigate isotope-selective interactions and evaluate process performance. You will analyse data, interpret results and apply scientific insight to solve complex technical challenges, helping to guide future research activities. Working closely with engineers, physicists and technical specialists, you will integrate laser systems with vacuum equipment, diagnostics, control systems and wider facility infrastructure. You will also contribute to project planning, equipment procurement, commissioning and technical reporting, taking responsibility for delivering key milestones and maintaining high standards of safety, quality and reproducibility. As part of an industrially funded programme, you will work closely with private-sector partners and suppliers, contributing to technical reviews and ensuring research outputs are delivered to professional and commercial standards. Success in the role requires the ability to operate effectively within a collaborative environment while balancing scientific excellence, project delivery, confidentiality and stakeholder expectations. You should apply if: You have a PhD, or equivalent research experience, in physics, optical engineering or a related discipline, together with significant hands-on experience in laser physics, optics and spectroscopy. You enjoy solving challenging experimental problems and have experience designing, commissioning and troubleshooting complex laser systems. You will be highly organised, collaborative and capable of working independently while delivering results in a fast-paced research environment. Strong communication skills and the ability to build productive relationships with industrial partners are essential, as is a commitment to laboratory safety, research quality and technical excellence. This role would suit someone excited by the opportunity to help develop a unique UK capability in laser-enabled isotope science. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term for up to 11 months Work pattern: 1 FTE Grade: J Salary: £44,746 to £50,253 per annum School/Unit: School of Physics Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 21/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Jacek Wasik, jacek.wasik@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391679": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Protein Engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108737 The role: We are seeking a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join Professor Ashley Toye’s group in the School of Biochemistry at the University of Bristol. This full-time position (35 hours per week) is offered on a six-month fixed-term contract funded by the Defence Science and Technology Laboratory (Dstl). The Toye group studies red blood cell biology and develops approaches that use red blood cells as platforms for biologically active proteins. You will contribute to a collaborative feasibility study at the interface of protein engineering, biochemistry and red blood cell biology. What will you be doing?: You will design, produce and characterise recombinant proteins and investigate their interactions with red blood cells. The work will involve molecular cloning, bacterial protein expression, affinity and size exclusion chromatography, and biochemical activity assays. You will assess cell binding and retention using flow cytometry and microfluidic flow systems, with mammalian protein expression where required. You will plan and troubleshoot experiments, analyse quantitative data, maintain accurate records and contribute to project meetings, progress updates and the final report. You should apply if: You hold a PhD in biochemistry, molecular biology, protein engineering, synthetic biology or a related discipline, or have submitted your thesis with the award expected before the appointment begins. You have practical experience of recombinant DNA methods, bacterial protein expression and protein purification, and can design experiments and interpret quantitative results. You can organise your work to meet deadlines and communicate clearly within a collaborative team. Experience of red blood cell biology, flow cytometry, mammalian expression, enzyme assays, microfluidics or computational protein design would be an advantage. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed-term funding until 31/03/2027 Work pattern: Full-time (100% time) Grade: J Salary: £44,746 - £50,253 p.a School/Unit: School of Biochemistry and Biomedical Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 25th October 2026 For informal queries please contact: Prof Ashley Toye (ash.m.toye@bristol.ac.uk) Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391694": {
      "title": "Specialist Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113771 The role: The School of Chemistry is seeking a highly motivated and skilled Specialist Research Technician to provide expert technical support across a diverse portfolio of research activities within Physical Chemistry and shared research facilities. This is an exciting opportunity to support internationally recognised research by working closely with academic staff, researchers and students across a range of disciplines including atmospheric chemistry, aerosol science, electrochemistry, colloid and formulation science, mass spectrometry, laser chemistry and materials research. The successful candidate will play a key role in the operation, maintenance and development of specialist laboratory facilities and advanced analytical instrumentation. You will provide technical expertise to support research projects, manage laboratory equipment and infrastructure, develop safe working practices and train researchers in the effective use of specialist facilities. This role is ideally suited to someone who enjoys solving technical challenges, working with complex scientific equipment and supporting cutting-edge research in a collaborative academic environment. Please note due to the nature of the role we require the role holder to be based primarily on campus What will you be doing?: Supporting research staff and students through the operation, maintenance and troubleshooting of specialist analytical instrumentation. Managing and maintaining laboratory facilities to ensure equipment is safe, reliable and available for research use. Installing, commissioning and servicing scientific equipment and associated laboratory infrastructure. Training and advising researchers on the safe and effective use of specialist instrumentation. Working closely with academic staff to develop technical solutions that support research projects. Ensuring compliance with health and safety requirements through the development of safe working practices and documentation. You should apply if: You have experience working with advanced scientific instrumentation in a research, technical or 3 years plus laboratory environment. You have strong practical problem-solving skills and enjoy diagnosing and resolving technical issues. You have experience supporting laboratory users and delivering technical training. You can manage a varied workload and work effectively with multiple stakeholders. You have a commitment to safe working practices and high-quality service delivery. You enjoy working collaboratively and want to contribute to world-leading research. Desirable: Experience with chromatography, mass spectrometry or other analytical instrumentation. Experience with gas handling systems, vacuum systems or laboratory infrastructure. Experience supporting research laboratories or shared scientific facilities. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term for 12 months (secondment cover) Work pattern: Full time Grade: G Salary: £32,080 - £35,608 per annum School/Unit: School of Chemistry This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 25/10/26 For informal queries please contact: Charlotte Edom-Hodge, Head of Operations Email : charlotte.edom-hodge@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391561": {
      "title": "Talent Academy Coach",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Centre for Sport Exercise and Health Reference: SUPP113812 The role: The University of Bristol is a top 10 university for sport. The Sport, Exercise & Health Department works with more than 70 student sport clubs, encouraging participation, enhancing the student experience & pursuing athletic performance, whilst supporting Academic excellence. The Sport Exercise & Health Department (SEH) also fosters strong community links, partnering with local schools, clubs & national governing bodies to deliver programmes from participation through to performance for the Bristol community. The #Wearebristol hashtag demonstrates our ambition beyond university life, we want to be an organisation that can represent Bristol, that the city can be proud of. Leveraging the university performance environment, utilising high quality coaching & athlete development as a proud ‘independent’ organisation, the University of Bristol Talent Academy seeks to develop & support the widest range of young people in hockey in the Bristol area. The Hockey Talent Academy, an England Hockey licenced Programme began life in September 2023 and is now in it’s second year of operation, following a highly successful first year bringing together 55-60 young people from across the region for in the order of 280 hours of activity, on-pitch training, strength & conditioning training, development & competitive matches, off pitch game education & video review, sports psychology & nutrition classes. The SEH Performance Team seek passionate, creative & highly skilled coaches to join our team. Building on established foundations, embracing the culture of excellence & people-focused development that exists today within both the Hockey Club & Talent Academy, the successful candidate will playing a key role in the continuation and enhancement of an inspiring, positive & supportive development focused performance culture at the hockey Talent Academy, mirroring that of the wider University of Bristol hockey & performance culture. What will you be doing?: The post-holder will support delivery of ‘day-to-day’ coaching for one of the Boys / Girls groups of c.25 athletes within the 4 hours per week. You will deliver quality individual & team focused coaching according to the England Hockey Talent System Framework & in line with planning developed by the University of Bristol Head of Hockey, Talent Academy Lead Coach &/or Talent Academy Group lead. The successful candidate will Deliver high-quality high intensity, person-centred coaching suitable for young athletes. Sessions to be game-based, representative learning, utilising other elements of the session design toolkit as appropriate. Assist / coach / umpire matches in support of training groups. Utilise Individual Development Plans to shape targeted coaching interventions. Use video & other tools as part of this. Provide detailed, developmental feedback to athletes that reflects both individuals learning objectives & observed performance. Support formal written feedback processes as well as informal feedback integrated within sessions. Contribute to fair and open, person focused selection processes, supporting all athletes with the same opportunities and an individual approach. Lead, work alongside lead coach & the wider coaching team, embodying a culture of continuous improvement, development & teamwork. Be part of planning meetings including for training planning, squad selection & communicate regularly with the Lead Coach / TA Head Coach. Communicate with participants & key stakeholders within their networks in an open, supportive, positive, collaborative manner, You should apply if: The successful candidate should Bring extensive experience of developing high potential (young) players in club & performance environments & ideally significant experience working within the England Hockey Talent System or former player Pathway. Demonstrate a track record of connecting with young people, parents & other coaches and as part of athlete personal development & wellbeing. Have active accredited coach status with England Hockey, holding at minimum England Hockey Level 2 Coach Award, alongside an ability to demonstrate continuous personal development beyond this. Hold an up to date Safeguarding Certificate. Demonstrate experience of team-working / co-coaching with other coaches to deliver a safe, prosperous & high quality development environment. Be an excellent, rounded leader of people, focusing on developing a long-term culture of learning & performance. Be passionate about sport as part of a rounded lifestyle. Be conscientious, committed, punctual & reliable. Demonstrate excellent verbal & written communication skills. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time, 4 hours Grade: F Salary: £28,778 - £32,080 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Centre for Sport Exercise & Health This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 18/10/2026 The University SEH Performance team is more than happy to discuss the role informally prior to the application deadline. Please contact Martin Hicks ( martin.hicks@bristol.ac.uk ) with any questions. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391605": {
      "title": "University TSS - Admin and Clerical pool",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/10/2026 Department: Human Resources Reference: Tss100011 The role: The Temporary Staffing Service (TSS) provides temporary administrative and clerical work opportunities across the University of Bristol. We have a variety of full time and part time assignments covering a wide range of skills in a variety of Faculties, Schools and Divisions. Joining the TSS will give you a chance to join an organisation with an exciting and thriving work environment, allowing you to develop your skills and experience. What will you be doing?: You will need to apply to register with the TSS and if successful you will join our pool. This means that as assignments come up that match your skills, experience and indicated availability we will let you know to see whether you’re interested. For more information about the TSS please see our website: Admin and clerical | Temporary Staffing Service | University of Bristol You should apply if: You have strong admin and clerical skills, and you would like the flexibility and variety that temporary work can offer. You are able to provide a clear and concise CV, preferably no more than 2 pages, clearly demonstrating your administrative skills and experience. You are available to start working immediately. We are currently only looking for applicants with full time availability."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391623": {
      "title": "Widening Participation Recruitment Officer (Race and Ethnicity)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Education and Student Success Reference: SUPP113753 The role: The role will be a key member of the Widening Participation team, helping to ensure the University effectively delivers on our Access commitments as outlined in our Access and Participation Plan (APP). The Widening Participation team’s purpose is to increase access to the University for students from disadvantaged, minoritised or underrepresented backgrounds, engaging with social and structural barriers and supporting the diversification of our student community. This is one of a team of Officers who deliver multiple projects, partnerships and initiatives to support a broad range of students and to increase applications and intake to the University. The role is varied and fast paced and involves working with a range of stakeholders across the team, the wider University, and in local and national schools and colleges. The role is ideal for someone with an agile and responsive mindset who is looking for a challenge within a supportive team and who is interested in working in strategically important areas of recruitment and Widening Participation (WP). What will you be doing?: You will lead on a range of widening participation projects and initiatives, supporting and recruiting post-16 students from underrepresented and disadvantaged backgrounds, with a particular focus on recruiting Black and Asian students to the University. Your remit may include leading an admissions-benefit project such as Next Step Bristol; leading our APP Student Advisory Group; coordinating communications as part of our Offer Holder Horizons email campaign; and supporting other projects such as The Destinations Conference and Insight Into Bristol. You will also work with target schools and colleges, with an emphasis on engaging students from underrepresented groups such as Global Majority audiences and students eligible for Free School Meals. Working collaboratively with a team of Widening Participation Recruitment Officers and Mature Student Recruitment Officers, you will monitor application and intake statistics and interpret this data to develop appropriate Widening Participation activities. There are also opportunities to gain experience in a range of strategic settings, including admissions processes and procedures, designing and delivering recruitment activity, data analysis, partnership development and relationship management. As we continue to deliver the University’s 2025/26 - 2028/29 APP, our WP strategy will evolve and different areas of focus may emerge requiring the postholder to work across different remits in a collaborative environment. You should apply if: You are experienced working with marginalised, underrepresented or disadvantaged groups, particularly Global Majority heritage backgrounds, and are committed to driving equalising change for students from these groups You enjoy working in collaborative teams, working across a diverse portfolio of projects You are organised and details-orientated and have excellent time management skills, with the ability to be flexible and balance multiple work streams, adjusting to changing priorities You have excellent data and evaluation skills, with experience of designing and implementing evaluation frameworks, and enjoy analysing complex datasets to find innovative solutions You are resilient and positive, even when dealing with complex and challenging problems You are confident and assertive and can communicate well with anyone with your excellent relationship building skills You can deliver engaging, effective activities and events for large cohorts, both in person and online You have a keen interest in changing the University for the better and making it more accessible for underrepresented groups in the long term, as well as an ability to identify and action “quick wins” to make a difference to students in the short term Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time, 35 hours per week Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: Education and Student Success This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 27th October For informal queries please contact Sarah Almond, sarah.almond@bristol.ac.uk or Sarah-Jane Steele, sarah-jane.kinley@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391827": {
      "title": "Accommodation Commercial Performance Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113836 The role: We are looking for a Commercial Performance Manager to lead the commercial performance of the University's accommodation portfolio, comprising around 9,000 beds and generating more than £80m in annual income. Working within Accommodation Services, you will use data, market insight and commercial analysis to drive occupancy, revenue and customer outcomes across student, staff and short-stay accommodation. This is a new role, offering the opportunity to shape a growing commercial function and influence strategic decisions across pricing, demand management, marketing, product development and customer journeys. What will you be doing?: Analyse occupancy, demand, booking and market data to identify opportunities to improve performance. Develop recommendations on pricing, inventory management, campaigns and channel strategy. Lead commercial initiatives to address occupancy challenges and maximise revenue opportunities. Monitor market trends, competitor activity and wider housing conditions to inform decision making. Produce reports, forecasts and business cases for senior stakeholders. Work closely with colleagues across operations, systems, service design, admissions and finance to deliver commercial objectives. Develop and maintain commercial processes, controls and performance measures. Support long-term planning through demand forecasting, performance analysis and continuous improvement activity. You should apply if: You enjoy using insight to solve problems and improve performance. You are motivated by understanding what works, what doesn't, and finding ways to make things better for customers and the organisation. You might currently work in a commercial, analytical, operational or planning role and be looking for an opportunity to broaden your impact. We're interested in people who can demonstrate curiosity, initiative, critical thinking and the ability to influence others through evidence and strong communication. This is a new role for our service and we are open to candidates with a range of backgrounds and experiences. We don't expect applicants to have experience of every aspect of the role from day one. If you can demonstrate strong potential, commercial awareness and a track record of using data or insight to drive improvement, we'd love to hear from you. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Hybrid working is available with a minimum of two days per week on campus. Grade: J Salary: £44,746 - £50,253 per annum School/Unit: Campus Division - Accommodation Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Sunday 1st November For informal queries please contact: Caroline Wynne ( Deputy Director of Campus Services ) on caroline.wynn@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391757": {
      "title": "Catering Supervisor (Events)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113854 The role: Source catering is the University of Bristol’s award-winning catering brand and are looking for an enthusiastic and friendly individual to take the role of Events Supervisor at the university. The role will be working in one of our catering outlets which include 4 catered halls of residence, some beautiful events & conference spaces, 9 cafes, 2 bars and a food court and will suit someone who has experience in the industry and can demonstrate leadership qualities. We currently have vacancies within our team for experienced Events Supervisors. Our events team work across multiple sites, situated across various parts of the city delivering a range of events from banquet dinners, BBQs, conferences to canape and drinks receptions. The team also oversee all things catering and events within the University’s sports complex at Coombe Dingle, as well supporting our in-house delivery team. This is an amazing opportunity that provides work-life balance, training & development, working alongside a large and supportive team and involvement in exciting food & drink projects. The contract is 35 hours a week, spread over 5 days, with paid overtime, generous annual leave as well as extra paid days off for university closure days. What will you be doing?: You will be supporting the Conference & Events Manager and the rest of the team delivering a range of events around the university to students, staff and external clients. You’ll be responsible for the running of events, including setting-up and closing down functions, ensuring stock and equipment has been ordered and is ready for use, overseeing casual staff working your events and liaising with clients on the day. You should apply if: You have experience in retail catering or hospitality. Extensive experience in catering for various functions, whether small or large, casual or formal and high-profile. Highly organised and self-motivated, able to problem solve and take responsibility for challenges that arise. Supervisory experience of casual team, able to delegate tasks and ensure high standards. Hold a full UK driving license, that’s been valid for a minimum of 2 years Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: 35 hours/week Grade: D Salary: £24,685 – £26,093 per annum School/Unit: Catering Services Shift pattern: 5 days in every 7 This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 28th October For informal queries please contact: Luke Barnes – luke.barnes@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391783": {
      "title": "Cleaner (Sloane Robinson Building)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113861 The role: This is an exciting opportunity to work within our brand new flagship building in our new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus, right next to the historic Temple Meads train station. The Campus is the catalyst for further development within the Temple Quarter regeneration area – one of Europe’s largest regeneration projects and a historic moment for the city. The University of Campus Division has part-time opportunities for enthusiastic and motivated people to work in a number of busy and active cleaning roles. You will be required to work as part of a team and also on your own to deliver the high standards required by the University. What will you be doing?: Expected to maintain a high standard of cleanliness within academic departments, laboratories, libraries, and office areas to maintain a clean, welcoming, and safe environment for our students, staff, and visitors. You should apply if: Previous cleaning experience is preferred but not essential, as full training will be given. Benefits of working for the University 25 days of bookable annual leave per year plus 7 University closure days Automatic entry to a generous pension scheme Inclusive culture, valuing the diversity of thought, beliefs, and background. Staff discounts including annual bus fares, University gym and cycle to work scheme. Opportunities to build your skills and to progress your career. Supportive and rewarding working environment. Occasional paid overtime available. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time (17.5 hours) Shift pattern: Monday to Friday Grade: A Salary: £24,479 pro rata, per annum (hourly rate £13.45) School/Unit: Campus Division - Site Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Thursday 29 October For informal queries please contact: the Site Services Department on 0117 4283140 or Dennis Pike ( Assistant Operations Manager ) on Dennis.Pike@bristol.ac.uk . Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391796": {
      "title": "Education Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Education and Student Success Reference: SUPP113856 The role: At the University of Bristol, we are committed to creating an outstanding experience for students from all backgrounds. As part of our welcoming and inclusive community, you'll play an important role in supporting students from their first day through to graduation, helping them succeed academically and make the most of their time at university. What will you be doing?: We are looking for organised, proactive, and customer-focused people to join our teams and become Education Administrators. In the role, you will support students and academic schools through every stage of the student journey, from registration and induction through to graduation. You will be a key point of contact, providing guidance, information, and administrative support while helping to ensure that student administration processes run smoothly and efficiently. This is a varied and rewarding role. You will manage a range of administrative activities, respond to student enquiries, maintain accurate records, support key academic processes, and work closely with colleagues across the University to deliver an excellent student experience. Most roles are based on our Clifton or Temple Quarter campuses, close to Bristol city centre with excellent transport links. You should apply if: We are looking for people who can demonstrate that they: Enjoy helping others and will love offering amazing support to students Communicate clearly and professionally with students, colleagues, and other collaborators. Make connections, create a welcoming student experience and contribute to an inclusive and respectful working environment. Manage competing priorities while maintaining accuracy and attention to detail. Take responsibility for their work and understand the importance of meeting deadlines. Adapt positively to changing priorities and unexpected situations. Work collaboratively and contribute to a positive team environment. Handle sensitive and confidential information professionally. Previous experience within higher education is beneficial but not essential. We welcome applications from candidates with transferable skills, strong potential, and a commitment to delivering excellent student experience. Additional information: For informal queries about the role, please contact Matt Ellis - matthew.ellis@bristol.ac.uk Please note we have a number of opportunities available on both an open-ended (permanent) and fixed-term contract basis. We reserve the right to close this advert early if sufficient applications are received. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391794": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Economics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Arts, Law and Social Sciences Reference: ACAD108752 The role: The School of Economics at the University of Bristol is seeking to fill multiple academic posts with outstanding scholars to expand the school’s academic and teaching profile in all fields, with special attention to researchers with extensive research or teaching expertise in data science and AI. We are looking to appoint scholars at lectureship level (assistant professors) who will contribute to our strong economics research profile, and who will show a commitment to excellence in teaching and to contributing to the research and education culture in the School and beyond. Posts are open both at the research focused Pathway One (Research/Teaching) and teaching focused Pathway Three (Education/Scholarship). The salary will be competitive and the University currently operates a Recruitment and Retention (i.e. market supplement) policy. What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will be engaged in research, teaching, and administration. You should apply if: The post holder for the Pathway One position should have a strong research background with evidence of an ability to publish in the very top international economic journals, and an aptitude to teach with expertise and excellence at both Undergraduate and Postgraduate level . The post holder f or the Pathway Three position should have extensive teaching experience at the Undergraduate level, evidence on innovative pedagogy and experience in teaching administration. Candidates should have completed or be close to completing, a PhD in Economics or a related discipline by the post start date (September 2027). Additional information: To complete your application, you will need to submit your CV, cover letter, job market paper and three reference letters via the EconJobMarket website This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 15th November 2026. For informal queries please contact: Professor Arpad Abraham econ-hos@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391743": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Forecasting",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Arts, Law and Social Sciences Reference: ACAD108732 The role: The University of Bristol Business School is seeking to appoint an outstanding Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Forecasting on a full-time and open-ended basis. This is a key appointment within the Business School, and we are looking to recruit ambitious academics whose research will help build and strengthen the School’s research capabilities in this important field. Appointments will be made at Lecturer or Senior Lecturer level according to the successful candidate's experience, achievements and academic standing. The University of Bristol is one of the UK's leading research-intensive universities, ranked 51st globally in the QS World University Rankings 2026. The University of Bristol Business School is one of the UK's largest and most successful business schools, bringing together a vibrant community of academics, professional services staff, doctoral researchers and students. The School has a strong commitment to producing high quality, engaged research, with and increasing emphasis on challenge-led interdisciplinary research. In 2026, the School relocated to the University’s new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus , joining colleagues in Engineering, Computer Science, Bristol Innovations and the Centre for Innovation and Entrepreneurship. Successful candidates will be based in the Technology and Operations Group , a vibrant and supportive academic community with an international reputation for research excellence, external engagement and impact, and outstanding teaching. What will you be doing?: You will develop (Lecturer) or lead (Senior Lecturer) an ambitious programme of research in Forecasting that advances business and management scholarship. You will publish high-quality research, engage with industry, policy makers and other external stakeholders, and contribute to (Lecturer) or lead (Senior Lecturer) the development of collaborative research initiatives and externally funded research. You will deliver inspiring and innovative teaching at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, including teaching in Artificial Intelligence, Business Analytics, Forecasting, and related areas. You will contribute to (Lecturer) or lead (Senior Lecturer) the design, development, enhancement and review of teaching units and programmes in Artificial Intelligence, Business Analytics, Forecasting and related areas. As we continue to expand our interdisciplinary programmes, experience of designing curricula and teaching collaboratively with colleagues from other disciplines would be an advantage. You should apply if: You have expertise in Forecasting, with research interests that complement and enhance the School's research profile. Your research combines methodological rigour with practical relevance and contributes to business and management scholarship. You pursue and develop (Lecturer) or lead (Senior Lecturer) an ambitious programme of research in Forecasting, while addressing important business and societal challenges. For Lecturer appointments, you should demonstrate the potential to develop an internationally recognised research profile, together with a growing record of high-quality publications and a strong pipeline of research, including published, forthcoming and planned work. For Senior Lecturer appointments, you should have an internationally recognised research profile, a strong publication record, a robust pipeline of high-quality research, and experience of securing external research funding, mentoring colleagues and contributing to academic leadership. We welcome forecasting researchers working with statistical and econometric approaches, artificial intelligence and machine/deep learning methods, and behavioural and judgmental approaches to forecasting. Applications may span areas such as business, economics, finance, operations and supply chains, as well as other organisational and societal contexts. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: 100% time Grade: J / k / L Salary: In the range of £44,746 - £50,253 (Grade J) / £51,753 - £58,225 (Grade K) / £58,225 - £69,488 (Grade L) per annum (depending on experience) plus a market supplement School/Unit: University of Bristol Business School This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 1st November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor L. Alberto Franco, Professor of Decision Sciences via email: alberto.franco@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391736": {
      "title": "Medical Receptionist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Education and Student Success Reference: SUPP113850 The role: We are looking for a Medical Receptionist to join the team and provide a friendly and efficient service at the reception area. You will help with the operations of day to day tasks and work with patient and members of the clinical team. The role is part time and you will be required to work 19.5 hours per week. What will you be doing?: Receiving telephone requests for medical appointments and fielding general enquiries about patient services Recording the movement of medical records in and out of the practice. Registering new patients and removing them from our list at the end of their studies. Completing a variety of administrative tasks supporting patient access and the transfer of medical information. For more information on the role, please refer to the job description You should apply if: Experience of working in a busy, customer facing role. Proven ability to work under pressure and with the minimum of supervision Effective communicator with a friendly confident manner Confident with IT Proven ability to work accurately and with attention to detail Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time, 19.5 hours per week Monday: 2pm-8pm (5.5hours) Thursday: 8.30am-2.30pm (6 hours) Friday: 8.30am-5.30pm (8 hours) Grade: D Salary: £24,685 - £26,093 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Education and Student Success This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 27/10/2026 For informal queries please contact Maria Geczy or Liz Fenwick 0117 330 2560 Maria.Geczy@nhs.net, L.Fenwick@nhs.net Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391824": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate / Research Fellow on Spectrum Access Innovation  x 2 positions",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108739 The role: The Smart Internet Lab is seeking two highly motivated, enthusiastic researchers of Grade I, J or K to research spectrum access techniques, focusing on automated and intelligent spectrum sharing technologies, looking at both technology enablers and policy consequences for wireless users, vendors, spectrum managers and spectrum regulators using JOINER National Spectrum Facility (JOINER-NSF). The successful candidates will carry out research for the JOINER ( https://joiner.org.uk ) and HASC ( https://allspectrumhub.org/ ) projects. What will you be doing?: You will work with the JOINER and HASC projects and will focus on spectrum access innovation with particular emphasis on automated, spectrum assignment techniques and the creation of a spectrum digital twin. You will also carry out experimental/proof-of-concept implementation work to demonstrate the applicability of your research. Your main research responsibilities may include: Conduct research into spectrum access, sharing and assignment, with a focus on automated and intelligent approaches. Collect and analyse spectrum data using JOINER’s national monitoring infrastructure, identifying patterns and developing datasets and tools to understand spectrum use. Develop and test spectrum-sharing techniques using software-defined radio and wireless system emulation across technologies including 5G/6G, Wi-Fi and satellite. Apply AI/ML and data-driven approaches to spectrum management and contribute to the development of a national spectrum digital twin. Contribute to the development and support of the JOINER National Spectrum Facility, working with researchers and external partners. You should apply if: You should apply if You have a PhD or equivalent research experience in a relevant area, or for Grade K, a relevant postgraduate research degree and substantial research experience. You have knowledge of spectrum assignment and allocation, spectrum policy and regulatory frameworks. You have experience with RF systems, wireless technologies and software-defined radio, ideally including USRP/GNU Radio. You have strong programming and data analysis skills, particularly with Python, C++ or similar, and experience working with large datasets. You have experience or a strong understanding of AI/ML, particularly its application to spectrum management or time-series data. You can demonstrate experience conducting research, analysing results and communicating findings through technical reports, scientific papers or presentations. You are able to work collaboratively across disciplines, learn quickly, take initiative and manage competing priorities and deadlines. For Grade J/K, you should also be able to demonstrate increasing research independence, publication experience and leadership appropriate to the level. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Please specify in your covering letter which position you wish to apply for. Position 1 – Open ended with fixed funding until 01/11/2028 Position 2 – Open ended with fixed funding until 31/03/2030 Work pattern: 35 hours per week/100% Grade: Grade I-K (subject to experience)/ Pathway 2 Salary: Grade I £40,704 – £45,641 OR Grade J £45,641 – £51,258 OR Grade K £52,788 – £59,390 per annum depending on experience School/Unit: School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering (EEME) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 03/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Xiaolan Liu , Lecturer - Email: xiaolan.liu@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391786": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate in Organic Geochemistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108730 The role: We are seeking to appoint a fixed-term full-time Research Associate or Senior Research Associate with experience in organic geochemistry, stable isotope geochemistry, and/or culturing phytoplankton. The project will begin around November 2026 and will run for 17-19 months. The postholder will be embedded in a research team led by Dr Caitlyn Witkowski within the Organic Geochemistry Unit, a world-leading research group in the field of organic geochemistry. Information on our policies and strategy is available on our websites, and we strongly encourage applications from all backgrounds. Please include a full academic CV and a brief cover letter (1-page) that includes i) a statement on how this post will build on your previous experience and what you will bring to this project . For further information, see: OGU: http://www.bristol.ac.uk/chemistry/research/ogu/ University of Bristol: https://www.bristol.ac.uk What will you be doing?: The project aims are to understand how carbon is taken up during photosynthesis, specifically how photoautotrophic phytoplankton fractionate and biosynthesis carbon, with implications for understanding the evolution of phytoplankton and how CO2 has changed across geologic time. You will be responsible for designing and conducting continuous culturing experiments using a chemostat bioreactor. Following the experiments, you will extract and analyse the phytoplankton lipids using analytical instrumentation (GC-FID, GC-MS, GC-C-IRMS, HPLC-MS). The aim is to identify the relationship between environmental conditions (e.g., CO2, light, pH) with the amount of stable carbon fractionation observed in the phytoplankton lipid suite. You will communicate the findings from this project to the relevant research communities through the use of scientific conference presentations and peer-reviewed publications. You will have opportunities to explore a wide range of organic geochemistry and biosynthesis questions, present at international conferences, develop your own scientific and interdisciplinary ideas, and work with both local and international collaborators. You will be supported to take on leadership opportunities (e.g., student supervision) and have the freedom and resources to build your own career portfolio (e.g., via workshops, training, etc). You should apply if: We are preferably looking for someone with: A good honours degree (or equivalent) in PhD in Chemistry, Earth Sciences, Biology, Geography, or relevant subject A PhD in Chemistry, Organic Geochemistry, Earth Sciences, Geomicrobiology, Biology, or related disciplines The ability to pro-actively and creatively implement wet chemistry and instrumental solutions to complex problems Relevant experience of designing and implementing laboratory experiments, preferably analysing the lipid content in phytoplankton Experience with environmental isotopes Evidence of effective science communication through publications of scientific papers in peer-reviewed journals Evidence of effective science communication through (inter)national conference presentations Experience of liaison with (external) collaborators and organisations Evidence for involvement in outreach activities Appointment at the Senior Research Associate level will require previous experience in research and (under)graduate supervision at PDRA level or equivalent industry-level, as well as experience in leading independent project management Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term until 06/08/2027 Work pattern: 1 FTE/35 hours per week Grade: I/J Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 (Grade I) £44,746 - £50, 253 (Grade J) Per annum School/Unit: School of Earth Sciences Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 29th October For informal queries please contact: Caitlyn Witkowski, caitlyn.witkowski@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391818": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Equitable Energy Systems",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108741 The role: This is an opportunity to work within the ambitious and impactful SHINE project, bringing together an international, transdisciplinary team to understand and improve the design of energy systems to have a positive impact on well-being and inclusion in The Gambia and Ghana. Working with academic institutions, internationally recognised organisations, and government ministries in Ghana, The Gambia, South Africa, Nepal and Brazil, the project will research and develop design methodologies and pilot energy interventions that seek to drive an inclusive energy transition. What will you be doing?: The role holder will closely collaborate with the international partners to implement pilot energy systems in The Gambia and Ghana, which will include overseas fieldwork. The role holder will follow a mixed-method approach, collecting and examining predominantly qualitative data (including but not limited to interviews, focus groups, participatory mapping) from the pilot energy systems. In addition, they will be integral in designing and refining the holistic SHINE framework incorporating data from the pilot energy systems. They will work closely with the broader SHINE team to understand the implications of the data and develop methods to further understand the linkages between energy systems and well-being. You should apply if: You will have a strong background in qualitative social science, ideally within the energy access sector, with the ability to conduct participatory methods as well as code, analyse and draw out key themes from interviews, focus groups, and transect walks that can be used to inform energy system design and future policy. You will also be able to analyse quantitative data, with the ability to use programmes like SPSS, MATLAB, REDCap and Microsoft Excel to draw out statistical measures. You should have experience working with international, transdisciplinary teams, so you must be able to communicate clearly, effectively, and respectfully across cultures, languages and disciplines. You will need to support the project administration, including developing ethical applications and ensuring that the project adheres to relevant University of Bristol requirements for research studies as well as necessary reporting. You will likely need to travel as part of this post, with visits to the partners in The Gambia and Ghana to collaborate on the pilot studies. Previous international travel experience is advantageous. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding ( 01/01/2027 to 31/12/2027) Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I/pathway 2 Salary: £40,704 - £45,641 per annum School/Unit: School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 4th November 2026 The interviews will take place shortly after the closing date For informal queries please contact: Dr Charlotte Ray: Charlotte.Ray@bristol.ac.uk or Dr Sam Williamson: Sam.Williamson@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391790": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Law",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Arts, Law and Social Sciences Reference: ACAD108719 The role: The University of Bristol Law School is a leading UK law school with an international reputation for outstanding teaching and research. Ranked 3rd in THE analysis of legal research in REF2021, and home to a diverse and inclusive academic community aspiring to the highest standards in legal education and research. Housed in the iconic Wills Memorial Building, the Bristol Law School represents a unique blend of tradition and innovation, offering an educational experience which challenges and empowers students and a research environment in which academic originality and excellence flourish . The School is seeking to recruit a Senior Lecturer in Law who demonstrates a commitment to the highest standards of clinical legal education to join our thriving academic community. The post is located on Pathway 3 of the University of Bristol’s academic pathways and will be appointed at Profile Level 3D1, Senior Lecturer, grade L. What will you be doing?: You will contribute fully to the life of the School and Faculty through teaching on our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, as well as supervising students working in the law clinic. Embracing excellence in all its forms, the School welcomes applications from those with relevant teaching and legal practice experience in areas of relevance to the Law Clinic; housing law, employment law, social security law, community care, family law, inquests, contract/consumer law, or civil litigation. You should apply if: You are qualified as a solicitor or barrister in England and Wales, or as a Foreign Lawyer registered with the SRA (Registered Foreign Lawyer), with a minimum of five years in practice and possess demonstrable ability in legal case management and experience in teaching and nurturing aspiring lawyers. The full-time post is available from 1 st December or asap thereafter; the School is open to requests for part-time working. The post is located on Career Pathway 3 of the University of Bristol’s academic pathways and will be appointed at Profile Level 3d1, Senior Lecturer, grade L of the University’s Academic Pathways . Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full-time Grade: L Salary: £58,225 - £69,488 per annum School/Unit: Law School This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 8th November 2026 Interviews will be held on Monday 23rd November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Catherine Kelly, Head of School, hos-law@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391779": {
      "title": "Senior Research Associate in Vegetation and Fire Modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108736 The role: The Postdoctoral Research Associate will work on the Copernicus Fire and Land-Atmosphere Modelling of Emissions (CoFLAME) project. CoFLAME will deliver scientific and technical advances to better account for interactions between wildfires and vegetation, improving estimates of wildfire emissions and natural CO₂ fluxes, while enhancing the forecasting of fire activity, fire probability, and fire spread. At the University of Bristol, you will develop new theoretical and modelling frameworks to quantify vegetation recovery, mortality, and ecosystem legacy effects following fire. This work will combine data synthesis, model development, and analysis to deliver new process-orientated insights into post-fire ecosystem dynamics and the role of vegetation legacies in shaping land-atmosphere carbon exchange and ecosystem resilience under future climate change. You will join the research lab of Professor Martin De Kauwe at the University of Bristol and will collaborate closely with an international team of researchers within the Copernicus Fire and Land-Atmosphere Modelling of Emissions (CoFLAME) project. Coordinated by the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF), the consortium brings together leading scientists from the Finnish Meteorological Institute (FMI), GEOVILLE, NILU (Norway), the Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute (KNMI), and the Portuguese Institute for Sea and Atmosphere (IPMA). Hybrid working is available and can be discussed at interview. This is a full-time (100%) position but appointment at 80% time would be considered. What will you be doing?: You will contribute to the Copernicus Fire and Land-Atmosphere Modelling of Emissions (CoFLAME) project by developing and applying new approaches to represent how vegetation responds to and recovers from wildfire. Working at the interface of ecosystem science, Earth system modelling, and remote sensing, you will investigate the impacts of fire on vegetation structure, carbon cycling, and ecosystem recovery. The role will involve developing and testing new model representations of post-fire vegetation dynamics, analysing observational and satellite datasets, and collaborating with international partners to improve the representation of fire-vegetation interactions in next-generation land surface models and forecasting systems. You should apply if: You have: A PhD (or soon to be awarded) in mathematics, vegetation modelling, physics, atmospheric science, or similar quantitative discipline. Significant experience in ecosystem, vegetation, land surface, or Earth system modelling, ideally with applications to wildfire, disturbance, or ecosystem recovery. Demonstrated programming/computing skills in a Unix/Linux environment. Strong research and publication track record (relative to career opportunity) Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Demonstrated ability to work effectively as part of a team and independently in a research environment. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended (fixed funding for 3 years) Work pattern: Full-time (100%) position but appointment at 80% time would be considered. Grade: J Salary: £44,746 - £50,253 per annum School/Unit: Biological Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Thursday 5th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Martin De Kauwe, Professor of Global Change Ecology via email: m.dekauwe@bristol.ac.uk Interviews will be held during week commencing 16th November 2026 Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391776": {
      "title": "Sterile Services Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: SUPP113871 The role: Bristol Dental School has moved to a city centre state of the art new site (1 Trinity Quay, Avon Street, Bristol) to deliver a new operating model and opened in Summer 2023.Applications are invited for a flexible, hardworking, and reliable sterile services technician for the processing of all dental instruments for this 119-chair clinical facility. You will be working within a small team, work closely with and taking direction from the sterile services supervisor and head of Dental Nursing and Decontamination. The decontamination unit will maintain the flow of dental instruments through the Dental School, the production of all goods received and dispatched to and from the clinical areas being a predominantly teaching environment for dental undergraduates and trainee dental nurses. What will you be doing?: You will: Work with a team of technicians to fulfil objectives within the teaching environment Undertake safe and efficient practices for decontamination, instrument assembly and sterilisation of instruments, consumables, and equipment within the predefined quality assurance standards. Engage with all aspects of decontamination and the decontamination process to include track and trace systems Work closely with the sterile service supervisor and Head of Dental Nursing and decontamination to achieve an efficient and fully functional sterile service department at Bristol Dental School You should apply if: You have: Previous experience as a sterile services technician or similar role within the sterile services field A positive attitude with good communication skills Committed in providing a high level of internal customer service Ability to work effectively and proactively as part of a sterile service team and within the wider dental teaching environment Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full time (35 hours per week) – Monday to Friday and p art time (14 hours per week) – Thursday and Friday Grade: D Salary: £24,685 – £26,093 per annum (pro rata for part time position) School/Unit: Bristol Dental School This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 29/10/2026 For informal queries please contact: Lisa Levitt, Deputy Head of Dental Nursing via email: Lisa.jones@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391815": {
      "title": "Student Money Coach",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Education and Student Success Reference: SUPP113640 The role: This is an exciting opportunity to help strengthen student financial wellbeing at the University of Bristol. As Student Money Coach, you will deliver 1:1 money coaching sessions, engaging workshops, outreach initiatives, and proactive interventions to support students in developing the skills and confidence to manage their money effectively. Working alongside colleagues in the Advice and Money Team, you will identify and support students at financial risk, contribute to University-wide financial wellbeing campaigns, and enhance access to financial education and resources. What will you be doing?: You will: Provide one-to-one financial guidance, helping students to budget, access funding, and navigate financial challenges. Support students at financial risk, including those affected by gambling, gaming harms, or other high-risk financial behaviours. Develop financial guidance materials, such as budgeting tools and tailored resources, ensuring accuracy and accessibility. Design and deliver financial education programmes, including workshops, outreach events, and digital resources. Collaborate with colleagues across student support, academic departments, and external organisations to strengthen financial wellbeing provision. Contribute to University-wide campaigns such as National Student Money Week and other initiatives to promote positive financial behaviours. Maintain accurate records of students supported and activities delivered to support robust impact monitoring and reporting. Monitor engagement and use data insights to evaluate impact and inform service improvements. You should apply if: You are: Passionate about supporting students to improve their financial wellbeing. Experienced in providing financial guidance, coaching, or support in a student-facing educational setting. Confident in designing and delivering educational or awareness-raising initiatives such as workshops, campaigns, or digital content. Able to communicate financial concepts clearly, both in writing and in person. Skilled in engaging diverse audiences and handling sensitive issues professionally. Organised, proactive, and capable of managing multiple priorities effectively. Confident using digital tools and social media to engage students and improve service delivery. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full time / 35 hours per week Grade: G Salary: £32,080 - £35,608 per annum School/Unit: Customer Experience This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Sunday 15th November Interviews are anticipated to take place on Thursday 26th November For informal queries please contact: Samuel Wood (Student Money Advice Manager) - samuel.wood@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391921": {
      "title": "Academic Representation Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Student Union Reference: EXTERNAL00455 : We’re looking for someone with the passion to support students in shaping and getting the most out of their university experience. In partnership with Bristol University, Bristol SU delivers an academic representation system of over 900 student representatives. You will be working to assist with the running of the academic representation system and working closely with the Academic Representation Coordinators to help them support academic reps in tackling issues they are working on and ensuring they have access to the support and resources they need. You will also record and promote the impact of the academic representation system to the wider student body, keeping them up to date about what we are doing to represent their academic interests while at university. What’s the job?: Role : Academic Representation Administrator Hours : 21 to be worked flexibly Salary : The starting salary for this role is £25,615 (pro-rata) Contract : Permanent Closing date : 2 November 2026 at 9am Who are we?: Part business, part charity, part membership body – Bristol SU is a seriously fun place to work. Our professional staff do something extraordinary every day: we support student leaders to give people a voice in their education, help people get the most out of their student experience and help people make the change they want in the world. We have over 32,000 members, 350 clubs and societies, a diverse range of professional services, and an innovative strategy that will help to create an inclusive and supportive University community. What will you bring?: You’ll have experience in managing competing priorities. You’ll be able to deliver effective administrative support for a busy team. You know how to communicate clearly. You’re a skilled planner. Why apply?: Because you’re excited to make a difference! We offer lots of great benefits including enhanced pay for parents taking maternity, adoption or shared parental leave, a competitive salary for the non-profit sector, a generous pension scheme and at least 40 days’ holiday. We’re committed to helping you achieve a healthy work-life balance through flexible working and are open to considering requests for part-time working. We provide professional training so that whatever your chosen field of work, with the right mix of challenge and support, you’ll gain fantastic experience, and room to develop and advance your career. We are proud to have been recognised through numerous accreditations and awards that demonstrate our commitment to creating a positive and supportive culture. We have appeared in the Inclusive Companies top 100 list and have won a number of awards for our commitment to diversity, including best Students' Union. No wonder 87% of our employees would recommend Bristol SU as a great place to work! Equality, Diversity and Inclusion are central to life at Bristol SU. We approach our work with positivity and celebrate our diverse and unique communities. Our people are proud of our values and care about creating diverse and supportive communities that are inclusive of age, disability, gender, identity, race, religion, sexual orientation and experience. Bristol SU strives to have an inclusive culture and an environment for all, underpinned by respect and compassion, where everyone feels welcomed, included, supported, valued and safe. Bristol SU recognises that candidates from some backgrounds/ identities may face additional barriers throughout their careers and when applying for new roles across the sector. We are committed to taking positive action to expand the diversity of our staff team by offering two guaranteed interview schemes – a scheme for people of colour and a scheme for disabled people. Be yourself, be proud of the work you do and build a career in a place that knows different is good. Key dates Closing Date: 2 November 2026 at 9am Shortlisting: w/c 2 November 2026 First Stage Interviews: 19 and 20 November 2026 Please visit our website to download our Applicant pack for more information before submitting your application. Click here to apply For general enquiries, please contact us at bristolsu-people@bristol.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391999": {
      "title": "Chef",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113725 The role: The University of Bristol Catering department (Source) have a rare and exciting Chef opportunity for the right person. We are passionate about serving high-quality, responsibly-sourced meals to our vibrant community of students, staff, and visitors. We believe in the power of good food to bring people together and enhance the campus experience. As a part of our team, you will have the unique opportunity to contribute to a sustainable and innovative dining environment that prioritizes the welfare of people and the planet. What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will prepare varied nutritious meals to a high standard specified by the Head Chef and Manager. Assist the Head Chef in planning menus for all meals, functions and special occasions. Meet the required standards of hygiene and food safety by monitoring cleaning schedules and food and equipment temperatures. Assist the Head Chef in the continual training of staff and induction and training of temporary staff. Assist in the preparation of orders for the purchase of provisions and monitoring deliveries ensuring deliveries are accurate and stores correctly. You should apply if: The post holder will be required to hold a recognised cookery qualification equivalent to at least NVQ Level 2 The post holder will ideally have gained previous experience in a reasonably wide variety of catering establishments, including hotel and/or restaurant work and preferably not restricted to the public sector; to understand the requirements of the differing customer types and to bring diverse experience to the University. A high level of culinary skill is essential to meet the wide variety of requirements, including student meals, conferences, functions and wedding receptions, day meetings and special meals. High standards of preparation and presentation are expected throughout. An understanding of the principles of food hygiene and the consequences of poor working practices is essential. Willing to learn and I have a genuine passion for food We know that kitchens can be a very busy environment to work it, and so it is essential that you have the ability to always work clean and tidy. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time (35 hours) Shift pattern: 5 over 7 days Grade: E Salary: £26,615 - £29,354 per annum School/Unit: Campus Division - Catering This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 9th November For informal queries please contact: Louise Rose (Catering Cluster Manager) on hrxlc@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392001": {
      "title": "Chef (Term Time)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113728 The role: The University of Bristol Catering department (Source) have a rare and exciting Chef opportunity for the right person. We are passionate about serving high-quality, responsibly-sourced meals to our vibrant community of students, staff, and visitors. We believe in the power of good food to bring people together and enhance the campus experience. As a part of our team, you will have the unique opportunity to contribute to a sustainable and innovative dining environment that prioritizes the welfare of people and the planet. The position comes on a term time contract (34 weeks a year, 35 hour/week), with paid overtime, 25 days annual leave plus 8 days for bank holidays and a further 6-9 days for university closure days. If you are looking for a role that offers a supportive work-life balance, opportunities for creativity and innovation, and the chance to make a real impact, we invite you to apply and become a part of our mission to create a better dining experience at the University of Bristol. What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will prepare varied nutritious meals to a high standard specified by the Head chef and Manager. Assisting the Head Chef in planning the menus for all meals, functions and special occasions. Meeting the required standards of hygiene and food safety by monitoring cleaning schedules and food and equipment temperatures. Assisting the head chef in the continual training of staff and induction and training of temporary staff. Assist in the preparation of orders for the purchase of provisions and monitoring deliveries ensuring deliveries are accurate and stores correctly. You should apply if: The post holder will be required to hold a recognised cookery qualification equivalent to at least NVQ Level 2 The post holder will ideally have gained previous experience in a reasonably wide variety of catering establishments, including hotel and/or restaurant work and preferably not restricted to the public sector; to understand the requirements of the differing customer types and to bring diverse experience to the University. A high level of culinary skill is essential to meet the wide variety of requirements, including student meals, conferences, functions and wedding receptions, day meetings and special meals. High standards of preparation and presentation are expected throughout. An understanding of the principles of food hygiene and the consequences of poor working practices is essential. Willing to learn and I have a genuine passion for food We know that kitchens can be a very busy environment to work it, and so it is essential that you have the ability to always work clean and tidy. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Term time only - 35 hours Shift pattern: 5 over 7 days Grade: E Salary: £26,615 - £29,354 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Campus Division - Catering & Events Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Monday 9th November For informal queries please contact: Louise Rose (Catering Cluster Manager) on hrxlc@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391935": {
      "title": "Clinical Lecturer in Primary Care Dentistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108748 The role: Bristol Dental School are appointing Clinical Lecturers in Restorative Dentistry, Paediatric Dentistry and Urgent Dental Care. This is an exciting time to join Bristol Dental School in our brand-new clinical facility. The role holders will play a key role within the School, contributing towards teaching on our clinical programmes. What will you be doing?: We are seeking outstanding individuals who have significant experience in general practice to help Bristol Dental School prepare our students for life in practice. As well as contributing to teaching and clinical supervision, the post holders will contribute to patient assessment and treatment on Restorative Dentistry, and Urgent Dental Care (UDC) clinics, plus there are opportunities to engage with other academic duties and development. You should apply if: We are looking for well-organised, enthusiastic individuals who have a keen interest in general dental practice, in delivering high-quality integrated teaching, and in contributing to the life of the School. You will be part of a strong cohesive team of enthusiastic staff. Applicants must be fully registered with the General Dental Council and hold BDS or equivalent. We have two fixed term maternity positions available on a Tuesday. The tables below set out the clinics that the positions will be on based. We are currently interested in receiving applicants who are willing to work on this day. AM PM Monday Tuesday 1x Restorative 1x Urgent Dental Care 2x Restorative Wednesday Thursday Friday Restorative and UDC: Fixed term end, 08 October 2027 Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term until 09/10/2027 Work pattern: 8 hours per week (0.2FTE) Grade: Clinical Lecturer (Dentist) Salary: £48,309 - £88,799 per annum (pro rata) School/Unit: Bristol Dental School This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 12/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Tom.Dudding@bristol.ac.uk - Restorative post Mark.Gormley@bristol.ac.uk – UDC post Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391995": {
      "title": "Facilities Maintenance Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113647 The role: Within our Building Services department we are looking for a new Facilities Maintenance Technician to provide preventative and reactive maintenance across our Residential Campus. In this role you will use your qualifications, skills and experience to carry out planned maintenance to help avoid issues and react to and rectify any faults across the campus. You will work closely with the Residential maintenance management team, Mechanical team and members of the Residential maintenance office staff to schedule and prioritise jobs whilst also responding to any emergency situations on a day-to-day basis. As a Facilities Maintenance Technician you will use the University’s building management system (BMS) to monitor and record data from critical plant and equipment across the estate. You will also respond and attend to faults and alarms automatically generated by the system. Building Services plays a key role in ensuring things work as they should for our staff and students. We aim to resolve any issues as quickly, safely and efficiently as possible ensuring customer satisfaction at all times. This role would suit an experienced mechanical fitter who is looking for a new, varied opportunity working with a friendly, supportive team. What will you be doing?: You will be responsible for providing support to the DLO, liaising with contractors, investigating and rectifying mechanical faults to all of the University’s building services. You will identify whether it is possible to solve the problem yourself, or whether you need to escalate the issue to the Maintenance Manager. The work is varied and can be unpredictable but examples of the day to day duties include: Site visits with the mechanical team to support with various issues Liaise with specialist contractors Resolving local heating issues Rectifying large and smaller heating system faults and failures Investigating water leaks from sinks, roofs, pipes, basins, waste pipes, toilets, showers, or baths. Investigating and repairing building drainage issues Responding to ventilation faults and performance issues. Fixing any hot and cold water generation and circulation issues. Performing planned preventative maintenance tasks. You should apply if: You have significant experience in the repair and maintenance of commercial building services. You will hold a recognised mechanical qualification such as City & Guilds level 2, Mechanical or BTEC, Level 2, domestic and commercial gas qualifications. You will also hold a full driving licence. Alongside your technical skills and qualifications you will be a team player with excellent customer service skills who is able to manage your own workload. You will also have a logical approach to fault finding and problem solving. Benefits of working in this role for the University include: 25 days of annual leave per year (plus bank holidays) Automatic entry to a generous pension scheme A group of family friendly policies including generous maternity, adoption and surrogacy leave options, the ability to take paid shared parental leave, up to two weeks paternity/ partner leave, leave to support fertility treatment and paid dependents/ carers leave Inclusive culture, valuing the diversity of thought, beliefs and background Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time - 35 hours per week Grade: H Salary: £36,320 - £40,704 per annum School/Unit: Campus Division - Building Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Monday 16th November For informal queries please contact: Joe Collins ( Maintenance Manager - Residences Zone) on bujfc@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391992": {
      "title": "Leading Cleaner",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113731 The role: This is an exciting opportunity to work within our brand new flagship building in our new Temple Quarter Enterprise Campus, right next to the historic Temple Meads train station. The Campus is the catalyst for further development within the Temple Quarter regeneration area – one of Europe’s largest regeneration projects and a historic moment for the city. The University of Campus Division has part-time opportunities for enthusiastic and motivated people to work in a number of busy and active cleaning roles. You will be required to work as part of a team and also on your own to deliver the high standards required by the University. What will you be doing?: Expected to maintain a high standard of cleanliness within academic departments, laboratories, libraries, and office areas in order to maintain a clean, welcoming, and safe environment for our students, staff, and visitors. You should apply if: Previous cleaning experience is preferred but not essential as full training will be given. Benefits of working for the University 25 days of bookable annual leave per year plus 7 University closure days Automatic entry to a generous pension scheme Inclusive culture, valuing the diversity of thought, beliefs, and background. Staff discounts including annual bus fares, University gym and cycle to work scheme. Opportunities to build your skills and to progress your career. Supportive and rewarding working environment. Occasional paid overtime available. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Part time (17.5 hours) Shift pattern: Mon - Fri, 17:50 - 21:00 Grade: C Salary: £24,479 - £25,179 per annum pro rata School/Unit: Campus Division - Site Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Monday 9th November For informal queries please contact: the Site Services Department on 0117 4283140 Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391861": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Economics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/12/2026 Department: Faculty of Arts, Law and Social Sciences Reference: ACAD108752 The role: The School of Economics at the University of Bristol is seeking to fill multiple academic posts with outstanding scholars to expand the school’s academic and teaching profile in all fields, with special attention to researchers with extensive research or teaching expertise in data science and AI. We are looking to appoint scholars at lectureship level (assistant professors) who will contribute to our strong economics research profile, and who will show a commitment to excellence in teaching and to contributing to the research and education culture in the School and beyond. Posts are open both at the research focused Pathway One (Research/Teaching) and teaching focused Pathway Three (Education/Scholarship). The salary will be competitive and the University currently operates a Recruitment and Retention (i.e. market supplement) policy. What will you be doing?: The successful candidate will be engaged in research, teaching, and administration. You should apply if: The post holder for the Pathway One position should have a strong research background with evidence of an ability to publish in the very top international economic journals, and an aptitude to teach with expertise and excellence at both Undergraduate and Postgraduate level . The post holder f or the Pathway Three position should have extensive teaching experience at the Undergraduate level, evidence on innovative pedagogy and experience in teaching administration. Candidates should have completed or be close to completing, a PhD in Economics or a related discipline by the post start date (September 2027). Additional information: To complete your application, you will need to submit your CV, cover letter, job market paper and three reference letters via the EconJobMarket website link below - https://econjobmarket.org/positions/12856 This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Friday 11th December 2026. For informal queries please contact: Professor Arpad Abraham econ-hos@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391889": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate In The Development of Novel Synthetic Boron-based Methodologies",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108745 The role: Three postdoctoral research associate positions are available to work in the Aggarwal Lab ( http://www.chm.bris.ac.uk/org/aggarwal/ ) to work on the development of novel synthetic boron-based methodologies. The three positions are subject to fixed term funding between the following dates: Position 1 - 1st January 2027 to 31st December 2027 Position 2 - 7th January 2027 to 6th January 2028 Position 3 - 11th January 2027 to 10th June 2028 What will you be doing?: The PDRA will be required to work on multiple projects simultaneously, but their primary responsibility will be on the design and synthesis of complex molecules, and the development of new synthetic methodology using boron chemistry. We will use lithiation-borylation to grow carbon chains as well as larger building blocks to make C-C bonds. The researcher will employ their synthetic expertise to advance the project. The research program will require a deep understanding of the processes involved together with strong grasp of fundamentals of organic synthesis for a successful outcome. The successful candidate will have excellent opportunities to develop supervisory, written, teaching and organisational skills and therefore this post provides ideal training for a career in industry or academia. Duties may also include helping with the training and supervision of PhD and final year project students. The successful candidate will be accountable to Professor Aggarwal FRS and to Professor Crump as Head of the Organic and Biological Chemistry Section of the School of Chemistry. You should apply if: • Recent experience in synthetic organic chemistry. • Be able to plan and execute experimental work involved in organic syntheses successfully and safely with minimal supervision. • The ability to carry out, interpret and properly record the results of modern spectroscopic techniques, including NMR. • Familiarity with online database searching e.g. Belstein and Chemical Abstracts for use in the selection and planning of experimental work. • Excellent written and oral communication skills • The ability to become part of a team working on the research project and to demonstrate the ability to work at this level, in particular to work supportively in a laboratory environment with junior co-workers as well as other research assistants. • The ability to present work in a clear and concise manner. • The successful candidate will be asked to submit written reports of their experimental results as required for the information of the supervisor. • A body of published work in peer-reviewed learned journals relevant to the advertised post. • The ability to meet deadlines and excellent organisational skills. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term. If you are applying for a specific position please note this in your application. Applicants who do not specify may be considered for all posts. Position 1 - 1st January 2027 to 31st December 2027 Position 2 - 7th January 2027 to 6th January 2028 Position 3 - 11th January 2027 to 10th June 2028 Work pattern: 35 hours/week Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: School of Chemistry Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Tuesday 3rd November For informal queries please contact: Professor Varinder Aggarwal, Telephone: +44(0)1174557797, V.Aggarwal@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391855": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate  in Intelligent Optical Network Architecture",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108743 The role: Develop the intelligent, resilient optical networks that future AI, cloud and edge systems will depend on. Join the University of Bristol’s Smart Internet Lab as a Research Associate or Senior Research Associate on HASC Phase 2, the EPSRC Hub for All-Spectrum Connectivity. Working with academic and industrial partners, you will develop programmable optical networks, intelligent automation and rapid recovery from failures, and explore new network solutions using hollow-core fibre. Our multidisciplinary environment connects optical and wireless communications, networking, cloud technologies and AI, giving you opportunities to turn research ideas into working demonstrations. What will you be doing?: You will develop architectures for metro, access and data centre connectivity; create automation, orchestration and resource-control algorithms; and investigate fault detection, reconfiguration and recovery. Sensing, telemetry and physical-layer monitoring will inform closed-loop control and resilient operation. You will prototype and validate hardware or software solutions through laboratory and testbed experiments, using infrastructure including JOINER where appropriate. You will publish and present your findings and contribute to partner collaborations, research proposals, standards and patent applications. At Senior Research Associate level, you will also take significant research initiative, support early career researchers or students and help coordinate collaborative activities. You should apply if: You will have a PhD, be nearing completion, or have equivalent research experience in optical communications, telecommunications, networking, electronic engineering, computer science or a related discipline. You should bring knowledge of optical network architectures; experience in network control, automation, orchestration or resilience; and practical experience of optical communication systems or network testbeds. Programming skills and evidence of research achievement through publications or relevant research and development experience are essential. We welcome complementary strengths across these disciplines. AI/ML, software-defined networking, automated recovery, fibre sensing or telemetry experience is valuable; expertise in every research theme is not required. Clear communication, teamwork and a willingness to learn are essential. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: open-ended with fixed funding until 31/03/2030 subject to funding – including the mid-term review scheduled for mid-2028 Work pattern: Full time/35 hours per week Grade: Grade I / J (subject to experience) Pathway 2 Salary: Grade I: £40,704 - £45,641 per annum Grade J: £45,641 - £51,258 per annum School/Unit: School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering (EEME) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 16th November 2026 The interviews are anticipated to take place on 25/11/26 onwards For informal queries please contact: Dr Shuangyi Yan, Associate Professor in Optical Communications and Networks Email: Shuangyi.yan@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391939": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate / Research Fellow  in Security Assurance for Agentic",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108749 The role: The Smart Internet Lab at the University of Bristol is seeking an outstanding researcher to join the newly funded AURA (Autonomous Unified RAN Architecture) project. AURA introduces autonomous AI-driven network management across critical network infrastructure, utilising AI agents that sense, decide, orchestrate, and propose configuration changes with minimal human intervention. This role focuses exclusively on addressing the critical security challenges created by the AI-native Future Networks architecture, ensuring the AI management layer remains trustworthy, secure, and resilient to compromise. The aim is to target agentic AI security assurance. You will be part of a team addressing the security and resilience of private 5G networks deployed across critical infrastructure sectors. The successful candidate will conduct novel academic and high Technology Readiness Level (TRL) Agentic AI security assurance research to characterise threat landscapes, conduct adversarial testing, develop runtime assurance mechanisms, and enforce zero-trust principles on the autonomous AI layer. What will you be doing?: Identifying and documenting the threat landscape across the data ingestion, intent interface, and execution layers of the Agentic AI system. Simulating and demonstrating agent misuse and multi-agent coordination attack scenarios on a live partner platform to establish a concrete attack landscape. Designing and implementing intent-to-policy mapping to translate agent intent into verifiable formal policy constraints. Developing a runtime decision validation engine to evaluate proposed configuration actions against cross-layer security dependency models. Implementing dynamic trust scoring mechanisms based on historical agent behavior and continuous behavioral monitoring across decision patterns and network-layer security impacts. Developing automated mitigation strategies without human intervention, including rollback mechanisms and agent isolation protocols for compromised agents. Publishing research findings in high-impact journals and presenting at leading conferences such as IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, ACM CCS, or USENIX Security Symposium. Evaluating research experimentally using the project partner's agentic AI platform and the University's JOINER national open-networks experimentation infrastructure You should apply if: You hold a PhD or near completion or equivalent R&D experience in computer science, computer engineering, networks, or communications. You possess an in-depth understanding of fundamental cybersecurity concepts, threats, vulnerabilities, and AI applications in network security. You have demonstrated experience in Agentic AI security in 5G and Beyond networks . You have a working knowledge of telecom standards and specifications, such as O-RAN, 3GPP, and ETSI. You have programming experience, preferably using Python, within a Linux/Unix-based research or development environment. You have experience conducting experimental research and technical investigations using systematic, evidence-based approaches. For higher grades (Senior Research Associate/Research Fellow), you should demonstrate substantial postdoctoral or equivalent research experience, independent research leadership, and specialist knowledge in areas like AI/ML security, agentic AI, zero-trust frameworks, and cloud-native observability tools. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open-ended with fixed funding until 29 February 2028 Work pattern: Full time/35 hours per week Grade: Grade I-K (subject to experience)/ Pathway 2 Salary: £40,704 - £45,641 (Grade I) or £45,641 - £51,258 (Grade J) or £52,788 - £59,390 (Grade K) per annum depending on experience School/Unit: School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering (EEME) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 05/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr. Rasheed Hussain, Associate Professor in Intelligent Networks Security Email: rasheed.hussain@bristol.ac.uk. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391980": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate for Advanced Quantum Networking x 2 positions available",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108742 The role: Join the University of Bristol’s Smart Internet Lab to develop next-generation entanglement-based quantum networks, focusing on scalable, reconfigurable entanglement distribution over fibre infrastructure. You will work with our quantum-networking team and academic and industrial partners across projects including, but not limited to, the EPSRC Integrated Quantum Networks (IQN) Hub, National Dark Fibre Facility (NDFF), Platform Driving The Ultimate Connectivity(TITAN). Appointment will be at Research Associate (Grade I) or Senior Research Associate (Grade J), depending on experience and track record. What will you be doing?: You will design, build and demonstrate quantum-network systems across laboratory, metropolitan and, where appropriate, national-scale testbeds. Research includes entangled-photon sources, single-photon detection, optical switching, wavelength routing, timing and synchronisation, and network monitoring and control. Where relevant, you will investigate Bell-state measurements, entanglement swapping, quantum teleportation and feed-forward control. Working collaboratively, you will deliver experiments, field trials, project milestones, publications and reports, and maintain safe laboratory practice and rigorous experimental records. At Grade J, you will take significant research initiative, develop techniques and methodologies, mentor less-experienced researchers and contribute to new research proposals. You should apply if: You have a relevant honours degree (or equivalent) and a relevant PhD (completed or nearing completion), or research experience equivalent to a PhD. You bring experimental experience in quantum communications, quantum optics, photonics, optical communications or a closely related area; practical skills in constructing, aligning, integrating and troubleshooting optical, fibre-optic and/or single-photon systems; and a sound understanding of quantum entanglement and quantum networking. You communicate clearly, work effectively in multidisciplinary teams, and can organise and prioritise research activities. Grade J applicants should additionally demonstrate independent experimental planning, complex data analysis, initiative and high-quality research outputs, alongside experience communicating research and developing collaborations. Please see the full job description for grade-specific criteria. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Position 1 - open-ended with fixed funding until 31/03/2028/ possibility of extension Position 2 – open-ended with fixed funding until 31/12/2027/ possibility of extension Work pattern: Full time/35 hours per week Grade: Grade I / J (subject to experience) Pathway 2 Salary: Grade I: £40,704 - £45,641 per annum Grade J: £45,641 - £51,258 per annum School/Unit: School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering (EEME) This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 9th November 2026 The interviews are anticipated to take place week commencing Monday 16th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Rui Wang, Associate Professor in Future Networks : rui.wang@bristol.ac.uk Alex Williams, Project Coordinator: smart-internet-lab@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391989": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate in Ai-Driven Downscaling Of Environmental Variables For The Health Community",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108757 The role: We are looking for a new postdoctoral research associate/senior research associate to work on AI-driven downscaling of environmental variables for the health community. The topics covered in the positions may include the following: Extreme weather events, such as heatwaves, droughts, floods, tropical cyclones Generative machine learning emulation of climate variables Climate model output analysis Exposure to climate hazards Climate epidemiology The successful candidate will be guided by the project Principal Investigators, Dann Mitchell and Emily Vosper. They will take a lead role in collaborating with the other institutions and project partners. The expectation is that they will publish 1-2 papers through the duration of the post. What will you be doing?: The position will be multidisciplinary and involve applying a diffusion model to climate model output to enhance the spatial and temporal resolution and using health data to calculate exposure for a variety of possible health outcomes including hospitalisations and maternal and birth outcomes such as preterm birth and low birth weight over the UK. It will be possible to also contribute to the further development of the diffusion model should they wish. The exact details of the work can be found in the project proposal, which is available on request from Emily Vosper. The position is funded until March 30 th 2028 (with a possibility of extension should bids for further funding be successful). You should apply if: You have the following: Experience in analysing large datasets such as climate model, observation or reanalysis output Evidence of scientific excellence A relevant postgraduate research degree or equivalent professional experience in the research area required Have given presentations at conferences and have some publications in respected journals or equivalent in the field Evidence of ability to lead peer-reviewed publications Excellent written and oral communication skills Ability to communicate complex information clearly, both orally and in writing Additional information: Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until 30th March 2028 Work pattern: 35 hours per week/1 FTE Grade: I/J Salary: £40,704 - £45,641 (Grade I) £45,641 - £51,258 (Grade J) per annum School/Unit: School of Geographical Sciences Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 23/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Name: Professor Dann Mitchell, Professor of Climate Science Address: School of Geographical Sciences, University Road E-mail: d.m.mitchell@bristol.ac.uk Name: Dr Emily Vosper, Senior Research Associate Address: School of Geographical Sciences, University Road E-mail: emily.vosper@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391875": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate in Neural Control of Cardioprotection",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108746 The role: A Research Associate or Senior Research Associate is sought to join a British Heart Foundation-funded research programme investigating the neural control of cardioprotection and cardiac microcirculation. The project addresses how metabolic and oxidative stressors influence cardiac microcirculation at the cellular level, and how central brain circuits mediate cardioprotection and can be harnessed to promote angiogenesis in the infarcted heart. The work spans in vitro, ex vivo and in vivo approaches, including viral vector-based circuit mapping, human cell culture models and surgical models of myocardial infarction and heart failure. The grade of appointment (Grade I or J) will depend on the successful candidate's qualifications and experience. The project is funded for 2 years initially. Hybrid working is not available for this role. What will you be doing?: You will plan, carry out and analyse experiments spanning in vitro, ex vivo and in vivo approaches. This includes performing in vivo surgical procedures (induction of myocardial infarction/heart failure, stereotaxic viral vector injections), designing viral vectors for neuronal circuit mapping, carrying out cell culture with primary cells and human hPSC-derived models, and performing advanced in vivo imaging (laser Doppler/laser speckle, echocardiography). A Senior Research Associate (Grade J) will independently plan and lead complex, multi-technique experiments, manage transgenic mouse colonies, and contribute ideas for the enhancement of techniques and methodologies. A Research Associate (Grade I) will carry out these activities under the close supervision of the PI. Both roles contribute to manuscript and grant preparation, literature review, and dissemination at scientific meetings. You should apply if: You are passionate about cardiovascular and neuroscience research and have proven skills in critical thinking and problem-solving. For appointment at either grade, you will have hands-on experience with in vivo surgical procedures in rodents, including induction of myocardial infarction/heart failure and/or stereotaxic surgery, and demonstrated proficiency in at least three advanced technique areas such as cell culture (primary cells, human iPSC/hPSC-derived models), in vivo models of cardiac pathology, advanced in vivo imaging (e.g. echocardiography, Laser Doppler/Laser Speckle, 2P), stereotaxic surgery and viral vector delivery, viral vector design, or Ca²⁺ imaging. A Research Associate (Grade I) will have a PhD in a relevant subject or be working towards one. A Senior Research Associate (Grade J) will hold a PhD, have postdoctoral research experience (or equivalent) in a relevant field, a track record of first-author publications in peer-reviewed journals, and presentations at scientific conferences. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended with funding until 30/09/2028 (start date – as soon as possible) Work pattern: Full-time (100% FTE) Grade: Grade I or J/Pathway 2 Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 (grade I), £44,746 - £50,253 (grade J) per annum School/Unit: Bristol Medical School Shift pattern: 35 hours per week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 28/10//2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Svetlana Mastitskaya ( Senior Lecturer in Cardiovascular Regenerative Medicine) svetlana.mastitskaya@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391977": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Cancer Biology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108760 The role: We are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic Postdoctoral Research Associate for a Little Princess Trust/Children’s Cancer and Leukaemia Group funded “New Ideas” research project entitled “Using splice-switching oligonucleotides for cancer therapeutics”. The project builds on our recently published work demonstrating the spliceosomal vulnerability of neuroblastoma [Bojko etal, Cancer Letters 2024, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.canlet.2024.217263]. We propose that altering specific mRNA splicing events may selectively kill cancer cells, or sensitize them to chemotherapeutics. The applicant will deploy targeted antisense oligonucleotides to select genes for this purpose. The post will be for up to 12 months (full-time). What will you be doing?: This project will require a strong understanding of gene regulation, in particular regulation by post-transcriptional mRNA splicing. You will be expected to show strong initiative and be responsible for the planning, execution, analysis and presentation of the project’s experiments. In addition to molecular/biochemical/cell biology techniques, the project may require expertise in bioinformatic analyses. You should apply if: You are highly motivated to impact childhood cancer research. You hold a relevant PhD, or expect to be awarded one very soon. You need to possess high-level molecular/biochemical/cell biology practical skills, including ChIP,RNA immunoprecipitation, co-immunoprecipitation, qPCR, alternative splicing analysis, western-blotting, tissue-culture, confocal microscopy, immunocytochemistry, flow cytometry. You should also have some hands-on experience of bioinformatics for proteomics and transcriptomics Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term Contract - 12 months (Specialist skills) Work pattern: Full time / 35 hours per week Grade: I Salary: £40,704 - £45,641 per annum School/Unit: School of Biochemistry and Biomedical Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 16th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Professor Karim Malik, Professor of Molecular Oncology via email: k.t.a.malik@bristol.ac.uk or tel: +44 (0)117-4550106 Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391857": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Volcano Deformation Modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108744 The role: The School of Earth Sciences a t the University of Bristol seeks to appoint a postdoctoral researcher from January 2027 to September 2027 to join the team working on the ERC Consolidator project, MAST, led by Prof. Juliet Biggs. The postdoc will build a nd analyse a systematic database of the spatio-temporal patterns of volcano deformation using the rapidly growing global archive of satellite imagery . What will you be doing?: Apply systematic modelling approaches to a global database of InSAR datacubes at volcanoes to build a systematic catalogue of source parameters, ensuring the results are consistent with other geophysical and petrological constraints on magmatic systems. Collaborate with the processing team to integrate modelling with the high-resolution workflow and provide feedback on the dataset development. Analyse the database using statistical and physics-based approaches to explore the implications for volcano monitoring and the behaviour of magmatic systems. You should apply if: You have experience with systematic approaches to InSAR modelling at a regional or global scale. You have experience developing quality control approaches for large datasets of automatically processed InSAR data. You have a strong background in volcanology and are able to interpret volcano deformation signals in the context of wider geophysical and petrological constraints. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term 04/01/2027 to 30/09/2027 Work pattern: 35 hours/week Grade: I Salary: £39,906 - £44,746 per annum School/Unit: Earth Sciences Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 2nd November For informal queries please contact: Professor Juliet Biggs. juliet.biggs@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391986": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Climate Change and Health",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108758 The role: The successful applicant will join the School of Geographical Sciences, but will work across the University and with cross-disciplinary research institutes such as the Cabot Institute for the Environment, the Elizabeth Blackwell Institute for Health, and Jean Golding Institute for Data Science. We are open to applicants in all areas of climate change and health, but evidence of strong connections and understanding of both the ‘climate change’ and ‘health’ sectors is important. We would welcome applications from candidates with interests in the primary application of data science and AI to research questions in climate change and health. Research interests might include: human health for different climate and adaption scenarios; fine-scale weather/climate hazard modelling relevant for health; different health, data, and AI models that complement and add to our existing ones, for instance related to lifetime exposures or impacts on chronic health outcomes; linked data and digital health; lived experience; different types of methods of assessment for climate and health impacts. What will you be doing?: Develop and sustain funding support for their own areas of research. Independently identify relevant research objectives for a research team to pursue and, where appropriate, contribute effectively to faculty research programmes, ensuring that outputs meet the quality and quantity expectations for the School and/or Faculty. Lead research funding bids and research teams. Take a leading role in the successful dissemination, nationally and internationally, of research findings. Develop knowledge of, and contacts with, appropriate funding bodies external to the University. Provide expert advice on research issues to research fellows and other colleagues. Play a leading role in, and/or help to create, networks of researchers active in their area of scholarship and help create opportunities for collaborative work with other research centres. Peer review manuscripts for publication and/or research bids. Participate actively in the development of the School’s research strategy. Make a significant contribution to the School’s research reputation and income. Develop effective activities to achieve engagement with research, and/or impact beyond academia. Where appropriate, develop relations with communities relevant to the research, and public and private enterprises in order to capitalise on intellectual property and collaboration. You should apply if: You have extensive academic experience, which includes contributions in research, related leadership and administration. Role holders will be expected to be leading collaborative research bids, research teams or scholastic projects (e.g. editing journals or academic books). You will have an established reputation within your academic discipline and will be producing research of national, if not international, quality. You will be making a significant leadership and/or management contribution within your school or the wider University. You will have completed your PhD and will have demonstrated excellence in scholarship, research, teaching, and administration appropriate to your career stage. The role of Research Fellow in Climate Change and Health is a fixed 3-year Pathway 2 position. We expect the role holder will develop their research including actively seeking opportunities to fund their research beyond the 3 years and publish their research findings, contribute to doctoral supervision, and support the activities of the School in an administrative role. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term for three years Work pattern: 1 FTE Grade: K Salary: £52,788 - £59,390 per annum School/Unit: School of Geographical Sciences Shift pattern: Monday - Friday This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 23/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Name: Professor Dann Mitchell, Professor of Climate Science Address: School of Geographical Sciences, University Road E-mail: d.m.mitchell@bristol.ac.uk Name: Dr Eunice Lo, Senior Research Fellow in Climate and Health Address: School of Geographical Sciences, 12 Berkeley Square E-mail: eunice.lo@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391915": {
      "title": "Research Manager (Data)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: SUPP113890 The role: The UK Longitudinal Linkage Collaboration (UK LLC) is a research platform that provides a secure computing environment to systematically link study participants’ data to their health, social and environmental records. This is an ideal role for someone with an interest in supporting the research community by managing, curating and understanding new and complex linked data and longitudinal data resources, and disseminating this through working groups and stakeholder liaison. UK LLC has developed a prioritisation strategy for collection of place-based/ geospatial datasets, you will work with a variety of data owners to acquire datasets and curate these for use in research. You will develop accompanying documentation and metadata to create research-ready data products. When researchers apply to access place-based/ geospatial data you will advise on the availability of the data and feasibility of the proposed work. There will be opportunity to contribute to the growing user-group, collecting feedback for continuous improvement of the resource. You will work with a suite of place-based/ geospatial data and have data programming skills which we will support you to improve. You will liaise with a wide range of external stakeholders in a fast-paced environment, whilst always working to the highest level of UK Information Governance Standards. There will also be opportunities to write pre-prints and papers, as well as present at conferences, workshops and other externally facing events as this role has a focus on stakeholder liaison and communications Part-time will be considered at 0.8 FTE. Hybrid working is available: 2 days a week minimum in the Bristol office, typically Tuesdays and Wednesdays. The interviews will take place on 17 th and 18 th November 2026. What will you be doing?: Providing researcher support and liaising between data owners' technical teams and the research community, leading working groups of UK-wide experts. Leading the organisation, curation, and supporting provision of new linked data assets. Leading and contributing to the documentation of new linked data to enhance research users' interactions with the data. Carrying out data processing/descriptive analyses to develop novel understanding, which will be published on GitHub or pre-print publications. Supporting the research community through working groups to share knowledge and maximise the functionality of these data for research for the public good. You should apply if: You have a background/interest in place-based or geospatial data, and you are qualified and/or experienced in working with a variety of data types, and the application of a variety of statistical packages and coding approaches. You enjoy applying these skills to new scenarios and solving problems whilst developing new methods and are keen to further improve your skills. You enjoy working in a fast-paced environment within a motivated team. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term maternity cover until 23/12/2027 or earlier return of the postholder from maternity leave/secondment opportunity for internal applicants Work pattern: Full-time/ 1 FTE ( part-time considered at 0.8 FTE) Grade: J Salary: £44,746- £50,253 per annum School/Unit: Bristol Medical School Shift pattern: 35h/week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 04/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Abigail Harrison ( Research Manager (Data)) Abigail.Harrison@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391975": {
      "title": "Research Support Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108753 The role: An exciting opportunity has arisen for a Research Support Assistant to join the multi-institutional PIONEER (Programmable In-Organellar Nucleic-acid Entry, Expression and Retention) programme, funded through the ARIA Precision Mitochondria scheme. The project brings together experts across disciplines to develop innovative approaches for engineering biological systems and organelles. Based in the School of Biochemistry and Biomedical Sciences, you will work closely with Prof. Ross Anderson, Prof. Mark Szczelkun, and a collaborative team of researchers. This position is ideal for a recent graduate or early-career researcher looking to build expertise at the interface of computational protein design and engineering biology. The role is designed to provide training, mentorship and research experience that will support future progression to Research Associate positions. What will you be doing?: You will contribute to the delivery of key research objectives within the PIONEER programme by applying computational approaches to protein and peptide design and supporting the generation and analysis of research data. Key responsibilities include: Designing protein and peptide binders using state-of-the-art computational methods and AI-based design tools, including RFDiffusion3, proteinMPNN, Boltzgen and DiffDock. Using structure prediction platforms such as AF3, ESMFold2 and Boltz-2 on high performance computing clusters to develop and optimise computational design pipelines. Performing molecular dynamics simulations to evaluate protein structures and interactions. Working with research software engineers and specialist computational platforms to support project development on the UK’s most powerful supercomputer, Isambard-AI. Collaborating with laboratory-based researchers to experimentally characterise designed proteins and peptides. Analysing and presenting research findings at team meetings and contributing to scientific publications. Maintaining accurate research records and contributing to project reporting and milestone delivery. Supporting and occasionally training students or colleagues in computational methodologies. You should apply if: You have a degree in a relevant scientific discipline and are enthusiastic about developing a research career in computational biology, protein engineering or related fields. We are looking for candidates who can demonstrate: Experience with computational protein design methods and associated data analysis. Experience of protein structure prediction tools such as AF3 and Boltz-2. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to present scientific findings clearly. The ability to work independently while recognising when to seek guidance. Good organisational skills and the ability to manage workloads effectively. A collaborative approach and enthusiasm for working within a multidisciplinary research team. Experience of high-performance computing, gene editing systems, bacterial defence systems, or research laboratory environments would be advantageous but is not essential. This is an outstanding opportunity to contribute to a high-profile, cutting-edge research programme while developing valuable computational and research skills in a supportive and ambitious environment. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed Term Contract (Specialist Skills) Work pattern: Full time / 35 hours per week Grade: H Salary: £36,320 - £40,704 per annum School/Unit: School of Biochemistry and Biomedical Sciences This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 2nd November 2026 Shortlisting will take place on Tuesday 3rd November 2026 Interviews will take place on Tuesday 10th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Ross Anderson ( Professor of Biological Chemistry) - ross.anderson@bristol.ac.uk / 0117 455 8937 Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391896": {
      "title": "Security and Campus Safety Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113765 The role: An exciting opportunity has arisen for a Security and Campus Safety Officer to join the University of Bristol’s Security Services team. A range of professional, security related training is provided. The role is a fundamental part of the University community and provides reassurance to staff, students, and visitors alike. What will you be doing?: You will be part of an Operational Security Team providing a service 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, that includes the following areas: Foot and Mobile Security Patrols Active engagement with a range of people including students, staff, and visitors Monitoring and response to critical and life safety alarms Operating a modern 24/7 Security Control Room Emergency 'First responder' services to a wide spectrum of incidents Car Parking enforcement Promote crime reduction and personal safety awareness across the University community Liaise with the Emergency services, as required You will work as part of a team of uniformed, well trained, professional Security and Campus Safety Officers working on a rotational 12-hour shift pattern working an average 35 hours per week over a 12-month period. The roster pattern is 11-hour shifts (plus 1-hour unpaid meal relief) comprising of 2 days and 2 nights followed by 4 days off (4 on duty / 4 off duty). The core shifts are: 0600 – 1800 (days) / 1800 – 0600 (nights). You should apply if: You have: Excellent communication and interpersonal skills The ability to remain calm and professional under pressure Experience in a customer-facing role, ideally in security Ability to dynamically risk assess any given situation Accurate recording of incidents An SIA Door Supervision (DS) or Security Guarding (SG) license is desirable for this role; however, we will readily accept non-SIA applications from candidates with transferable skills in areas such as Customer Service or who are interested in pursuing a security career. Full training and support will be provided to obtain the necessary licencing for this role. The following is essential: Full UK Driving Licence The following would also be useful, but are not essential as full training will be provided: SIA Public Space Surveillance (PSS) (CCTV) licence First Aid qualification (with 1 days additional leave on qualification) Experience in a similar role We would particularly welcome applications from women. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time Grade: F Salary: £36,692.50 - £40,902.50 per annum ( £29,354 - £32,722 plus 25% for rotating shifts) School/Unit: Campus Division - Security Services This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Tuesday 3rd November For informal queries about the role, please contact Garry Vine, Deputy Operations Manager, who can be contacted between 9am and 3pm on 0117 4561105. Please be sure to address how you meet the criteria from the person specification in your supporting statement. Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391864": {
      "title": "Senior Mental Health Adviser",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Education and Student Success Reference: SUPP113696 The role: We are looking to appoint 1x Full time, Fixed term, Senior Mental Health Advisor to join our Mental Health Advisory Service (MHAS)Team. The fixed term position is available from January 2027 – December 2027. MHAS is a team of Senior Mental Health Advisors & Mental Health Advisors, within the student counselling service. We are positioned within the Education and Student Success Division and work in partnership with the Student Wellbeing Service, Residential Life, Disability Service and Student Health Service. MHAS works particularly with students with severe and enduring, complex mental health difficulties. The Mental Health Advisors are a group of highly skilled practitioners who work 1-1 and in groups with students and key functions of the role include triage and assessment, formulations, care planning, brief skills-based interventions, liaison, risk management and risk reduction work with students. In line with the wider University strategy, we are committed to fostering equality, diversity and inclusion, and anti-racism. We particularly encourage applications from candidates who identify as male, come from or have significant experience of working with Black, Asian and minority ethnic communities, gender non-conforming communities and/or speak a global majority language (in addition to English). We are currently under-represented in these areas, although all appointments will be made on merit of the candidate. The Student Counselling Service is working hard to create a welcoming, exciting and joyful place to work, meeting the demographic of the student population and offering more diverse and inclusive therapeutic support. As a team we are genuinely passionate about equality, diversity and inclusion; it forms a key thread through all the work we do. We want everyone that joins to be a part of this and to contribute to the friendly and fun community we are creating. What will you be doing?: The MHAS team supports students with severe and enduring, complex mental health difficulties. As part of the team, you will work using the recovery model (hope, personal responsibility, education, self-advocacy and support) with students and are responsible for case formulations, care planning, risk assessments, and the subsequent risk reduction and stabilisation interventions. You would be working with awareness of the different needs of the student population such as neurodivergent students, international students etc. Senior Mental Health advisors deliver 1-1 sessions with students, cover MHAS duty function on a rota basis, liaise with internal and external partners, and lead on project work when needed. As a Senior Mental Health advisor, you will support MHAS staff around complex cases, assist in service risk management, and have a key role in supporting the development of the service. You will also be responsible for attending and contributing to a multi-disciplinary complex case meetings as well as responsibility for case reviews, service capacity management and allocations. The MHAS Team are part of the wider student counselling service and attend full team meetings, CPD training and peer supervision spaces. Additional individual and group supervision is provided by external supervisors on a monthly basis. You should apply if: You should apply if you are a qualified Mental Health Nurse, Mental Health Occupational Therapist, Mental Health Social worker or Clinical Psychologist, who holds current registration with a relevant professional body. The professional body you are registered with must include a responsibility to maintain professional accreditation through updating skills and knowledge, and one which upholds a code of ethics and a complaints procedure. You must be a registered mental health practitioner with significant clinical experience. You will have exceptional knowledge and in person experience of supporting people with complex mental health needs and have a good understanding of the specific needs of a diverse and unique population of university students. You will need to be able to work very independently, and at times with minimal direction, as well as working very collaboratively with colleagues to meet the needs and objectives the team. The Senior Mental Health Advisors are based on site, due to the nature of the work. MHAS operates Monday, & Tuesday 9am – 6:30pm Wednesday, Thursday & Friday 9am -5pm. We do not offer condensed hours within the Mental Health Advisory Service as our client facing hours line up with the key services that we liaise with to appropriately manage risk and referrals. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term until 31/12/2027 Work pattern: Monday 9:00am – 5:00pm Tuesday 10:30am – 6:30pm Wednesday 9:00am– 5:00pm Thursday 9:00am – 5:00pm Friday 9:00am – 5:00pm Grade: J Salary: £ 44,746 - £50,253 per annum School/Unit: Education and Student Success This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Monday 2nd November For any informal queries please contact Daniel Brownen daniel.brownen@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=391950": {
      "title": "Toolroom Machinist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: SUPP113889 The role: A fantastic opportunity has a risen for an enthusiastic and experienced Toolroom/Workshop Machinist based in the University of Bristol, Faculty of Science & Engineering Queens Mechanical Workshop. Manufacturing of bespoke designs, working in a toolroom environment with other highly skilled toolmakers. Providing specialist support to academics, Postgraduates and students across the faculty of Science and Engineering. Providing teaching support using the background knowledge of apprenticeships, to aid in the use of pillar drills and other equipment. What will you be doing?: Main responsibilities: Toolroom machining. Machines include CNC Milling turning and EDM. Manual lathes, mills grinding and laser cutting. Fitting knowledge/skills also. Teaching support in a classroom environment. Supporting research projects around Queens building and adding design knowledge. You should apply if: You have: An apprentice served tool room machinist. Knowledge of CAD systems, inclusive of siemens, fanuc and Autodesk/fusion Excellent communication skills Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open-ended Work pattern: Full-time/35 hours per week Grade: H Salary: £36,320 - £40,704 per annum School/Unit: Engineering Technicians Advertisement/selection timeline: Closing date: 11:59pm on Thursday 5 November 2026. Shortlisting: Friday 6 November 2026. Interviews: Thursday 12 November 2026. For informal queries please contact: Ricky Billingham, Technical Manager Email: rb14942@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392023": {
      "title": "Research  Associate in ultrasonic non-destructive testing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108735 The role: The Ultrasonics and Non-Destructive Testing (UNDT) research group is seeking a Research Associate who will contribute to project “3D ultrasonic imaging for characterisation of critical microstructural locations in polycrystalline materials,” which aims to develop a material characterisation framework based on using ultrasonic array measurements to create a volumetric map of local microstructural properties. This project offers a unique opportunity to contribute to cutting-edge research at the interface of materials science, ultrasonic imaging, and advanced signal processing with significant benefits and applications in aerospace, nuclear, renewable and conventional energy, and other industrial sectors. The UNDT group at the University of Bristol is a world leader in the application of ultrasonic arrays to NDE problems. The major initial development was the concept of Full Matrix Capture (FMC), which consists of recording signals from all transmitter-receiver array elements and then performing imaging in post-processing. This approach has completely transformed the use of ultrasonic arrays for NDE and is now available on most commercial platforms. Another significant achievement was related to the development of FMC based defect characterisation algorithms. This post is full time and has funding for up to 14 months. What will you be doing?: This role focuses on the optimisation and validation of ultrasonic microstructural characterisation methods. The successful candidate will perform experimental material testing using a range of conventional material characterisation techniques, generating datasets to support numerical modelling and ultrasonic measurements. You should apply if: The successful candidate will demonstrate strong expertise in materials science, ultrasonic imaging and signal processing, with a clear track record of research excellence and the ability to work independently within a multidisciplinary environment. Essential Qualifications and Experience A PhD (or near completion) in Materials Science, Engineering, Physics, or a closely related field Research experience in ultrasonic non-destructive testing Hands-on experience in conventional microstructure characterisation methods (e.g. Electron Back-Scattered Diffraction) Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding until 14 months Work pattern: Full time/35 hours per week Grade: I – Pathway 2 Salary: £40,704 - £45,641 (Grade I) per annum School/Unit: School of Electrical, Electronic and Mechanical Engineering This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 10/11/26 The interviews will take place shortly after the closing date For informal queries please contact: Alexander Velichko, Senior Lecturer +44 117 33 15920, a.velichko@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392015": {
      "title": "Research Associate / Senior Research Associate  in Visual Neurophysiology (Eye Tracking)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108755 The role: This is a fixed-term post providing maternity leave cover for a Postdoctoral research assistant who is running a randomised trial of a novel therapeutic app for children, designed to improve eye movement control. The trial relies on using an Eyetracker to assess the primary outcome and the postholder will need experience of using an Eyelink Eyetracker to test children, including children with additional needs; of using Matlab and PsychToolbox to set up the tests and of analysis of Eyelink data. The postholder will also need to assist the PI in preparing the study documents and in the day-to-day running of the trial. The postholder will work closely with the PI, the co-lead Professor Iain Gilchrist and the Clinical Research Unit team at the Eye Hospital. The post is available at either Research Associate or Senior Research Associate level. Some days in Eye hospital are essential for testing trial participants. What will you be doing?: Research Responsibilities will include: Eye-tracking children taking part in the EyeTrain study, alongside clinicians Supporting and training clinical staff on how to eye track the children and to troubleshoot any problems as they arise Managing data quality control to ensure everyone eye tracking the children are working to the same standard and identifying any problems Designing and managing preliminary data processing for the eye tracking data Drafting sections of study report for the funder and results papers You should apply if: You have experience using an Eyelink Duo to test children. You have a postgraduate research degree (PhD) in a related subject or are near to submission of such a degree. Additional information: Contract type: Fixed term for 6 months or earlier return of the postholder from maternity leave Work pattern: Part time / 0.5FTE Grade: I or J / Pathway 2 Salary: I: £40,704 – £45,641, J: £45,641 - £51,258 per annuum pro rata School/Unit: Bristol Medical School Shift pattern: 17.5 h per week This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on 03/11/2026 For informal queries please contact: Cathy Williams ( Professor of Paediatric Ophthalmology): Cathy.Williams@bristol.ac.uk , Iain Gilchrist (Professor of Neuropsychology): I.D.Gilchrist@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392019": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Hers Group",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Health and Life Sciences Reference: ACAD108750 The role: This is an exciting opportunity to join an active multidisciplinary research team, focussed on optimisation, synthesis and evaluation of PROteolysis TArgeted Chimeras (PROTACs) to induce targeted protein degradation in human platelets and evaluate their functional effect on human platelet function and thrombosis. We are seeking to appoint a talented, ambitious, and self-motivating Postdoctoral Research Assistant (PDRA) with a background in Biomedical Sciences (Biochemistry, Pharmacology, Medical Biology, Cell Biology etc) to join the PROTAC research team of Prof Ingeborg Hers (Bristol Medical School). What will you be doing?: You will work on a collaborative project within the Hers Group, funded by an external industrial partner, and will investigate the effects of BTK degradation on human platelet signalling, function, and thrombosis. You will compare the ability of a range of BTK degraders, alongside conventional BTK kinase inhibitors, and assess the resulting functional and signalling consequences. Work will involve studies on human platelets, as well as in vivo/ex vivo mouse studies to explore the effect of compounds on thrombosis and haemostasis. The project will provide comparative insight into BTK degradation versus kinase inhibition and its potential impact on platelet function and thrombosis. You should apply if: You have: A Biomedical background and a recently (or imminently) awarded PhD in a relevant area. Experience with a wide range of experimental techniques, such as cell culture, western blotting, FACS analysis and confocal microscopy, and are confident to develop new techniques/approaches. Experience in platelet functional analysis and animal work is desirable. Excellent problem-solving abilities and an ability to plan and organise your own workload effectively as well as working in a team environment. Good organisational, interpersonal and communication skills and ability to write and publish scientific papers (commensurate with career stage) are essential. We look forward to receiving applications from motivated, enthusiastic, and curious people with a keen interest in cellular mechanisms, novel approaches and problem solving. Additional information: Contract type: Open-ended (with fixed funding until 31/08/2028 Work pattern: Full-time Grade: I Salary: £40,704 - £45,641 per annum School/Unit: Bristol Medical School This advert will close at 23:59 UK on Tuesday 10th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Prof Ingeborg Hers, Professor of Pharmacology and Cell Signalling via email: i.hers@bris.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392046": {
      "title": "Future Research Leader",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Department: Student Union Reference: EXTERNAL00456 The role: ​Future Research Leaders – Develop your Skills and Transform the Local Community! Are you ready to make a real impact in Bristol’s community? Are you interested in research and social issues? The Future Research Leaders is an innovative programme for students to tackle real-world social issues while gaining valuable research and leadership skills. We are looking for six passionate students to join this year’s project “Who Moves Bristol?” exploring transportation in Bristol. As a Future Research leader, you’ll: Collaborate with a diverse team to co-create innovative research solutions alongside local organisations. Develop key skills through exclusive training sessions in leadership, research practice, and career development. Gain a “Future Research Leaders” Certification – a credential to boost your CV and open doors. Build long-lasting networks with professionals and peers invested in making a difference. If you’re ready to drive change, build connections, and challenge yourself apply now to be a Future Research Leader! We strongly encourage students from all backgrounds to apply, including Disabled, global majority, and LGBTQ+ students. What’s the job?: Role : Future Research Leader Hours : No more than 5 hours a week Salary : £500 (£100 every month from January-May) Contract : December 2026 – May 2027 Closing date : Thursday 12th November 2026, 12pm Who are we?: Part business, part charity, part membership body – Bristol SU is a seriously fun place to work. Our professional staff do something extraordinary every day: we support student leaders to give people a voice in their education, help people get the most out of their student experience and help people make the change they want in the world. We have over 32,000 members, 350 clubs and societies, a diverse range of professional services, and an innovative strategy that will help to create an inclusive and supportive University community. What will you bring?: Interest in Research & Social Change : A genuine curiosity about research and a drive to make a difference within the community. Strong Team Playe r: An ability to work effectively in a diverse team, contributing ideas and supporting others. Problem-Solving Skills : Creativity and resilience when tackling challenges or obstacles in projects. Openness to Learning : The willingness to try new approaches, open to learning from others, and generous in sharing what you know. Organised & Prioritisation Savvy : The ability to manage tasks and keep projects moving forward. Why apply?: Because you’re excited to make a difference! Equality, Diversity and Inclusion are central to life at Bristol SU. We approach our work with positivity and celebrate our diverse and unique communities. Our people are proud of our values and care about creating diverse and supportive communities that are inclusive of age, disability, gender, identity, race, religion, sexual orientation and experience. Bristol SU strives to have an inclusive culture and an environment for all, underpinned by respect and compassion, where everyone feels welcomed, included, supported, valued and safe. Bristol SU recognises that candidates from some backgrounds/ identities may face additional barriers throughout their careers and when applying for new roles across the sector. We are committed to taking positive action to expand the diversity of our staff team by offering two guaranteed interview schemes – a scheme for people of colour and a scheme for disabled people. Be yourself, be proud of the work you do and build a career in a place that knows different is good. Key dates Closing Date: Thursday 12th November 2026, 12pm Shortlisting: Friday 13th November - Monday 16th November Group Interviews: Tuesday 24th November For general enquiries, please contact us at bristolsu-people@bristol.ac.uk . Click here to apply"
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392048": {
      "title": "Merchandise Senior Coordinator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Department: Student Union Reference: EXTERNAL00457 The role: We're on the lookout for an energetic, outgoing, and motivated Senior Merchandise Coordinator to take the lead in our university apparel shop! If you thrive in a fast-paced environment, love engaging with people, and have a passion for retail and events, this role is for you. Based in our on-campus shop at The Richmond Building, you'll oversee operations across our online store and physical shop. We need someone who brings great energy, excellent people skills, and a proactive approach to driving sales and engagement. If you're ready to make an impact and be at the heart of student life, we want to hear from you! What’s the job?: Role : Merchandise Senior Coordinator Hours : 21 hours per week (Flexible working hours to be considered) – part-time role (0.6FTE) Salary : The starting salary for this role is £31, 465 pro rata Contract : Permanent Closing date : 13th November 2026 4pm Who are we?: Part business, part charity, part membership body – Bristol SU is a seriously fun place to work. Our professional staff do something extraordinary every day: we support student leaders to give people a voice in their education, help people get the most out of their student experience and help people make the change they want in the world. We have over 32,000 members, 350 clubs and societies, a diverse range of professional services, and an innovative strategy that will help to create an inclusive and supportive University community. What will you bring?: You’ll have experience in retail and event coordination within fast-paced environments—ideally with a background in managing both online and physical store operations. You know how to strategize and execute promotional events that resonate with students, while streamlining processes to support both the apparel shop and other related services. You’re a skilled communicator and leader who can inspire teams, solve problems proactively, and integrate creative marketing strategies into everyday operations. Why apply?: Because you’re excited to make a difference! We offer lots of great benefits including enhanced pay for parents taking maternity, adoption or shared parental leave, a competitive salary for the non-profit sector, a generous pension scheme and at least 40 days’ holiday. We’re committed to helping you achieve a healthy work-life balance through flexible working and are open to considering requests for part-time working. We provide professional training so that whatever your chosen field of work, with the right mix of challenge and support, you’ll gain fantastic experience, and room to develop and advance your career. We are proud to have been recognised through numerous accreditations and awards that demonstrate our commitment to creating a positive and supportive culture. We have appeared in the Inclusive Companies top 100 list and have won a number of awards for our commitment to diversity, including best Students' Union. No wonder 87% of our employees would recommend Bristol SU as a great place to work! Equality, Diversity and Inclusion are central to life at Bristol SU. We approach our work with positivity and celebrate our diverse and unique communities. Our people are proud of our values and care about creating diverse and supportive communities that are inclusive of age, disability, gender, identity, race, religion, sexual orientation and experience. Bristol SU strives to have an inclusive culture and an environment for all, underpinned by respect and compassion, where everyone feels welcomed, included, supported, valued and safe. Bristol SU recognises that candidates from some backgrounds/ identities may face additional barriers throughout their careers and when applying for new roles across the sector. We are committed to taking positive action to expand the diversity of our staff team by offering two guaranteed interview schemes – a scheme for people of colour and a scheme for disabled people. Be yourself, be proud of the work you do and build a career in a place that knows different is good. Key dates Closing Date: 13th November 2026, 4pm Please visit our website to download our Applicant pack for more information before submitting your application. For general enquiries, please contact us at bristolsu-people@bristol.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392035": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Senior Research Associate in Weather and Health x 2 positions available",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Science and Engineering Reference: ACAD108762 The role: We are looking for two post-doctoral Research/Senior Research Associates to join Dr Eunice Lo’s UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship team, which focuses on extreme weather, climate change, and human health. The Futureproof project aims to develop a new field of weather-health prediction science to enhance the climate resilience of the NHS. The successful candidates will run environmental-epidemiology models and develop weather-health forecasts for short- and long-term futures, focussing on extreme events including heat, cold, and storms. They will also investigate the human health impacts of unprecedented extremes and different climate change and socio-economic scenarios. Additionally, the post-holders will engage with a wide range of external project partners across academia, the Met Office, NHS, Cabinet Office, and national public health agencies. The post-holders will be supervised by Eunice Lo and join Dr Lo’s wider Climate and Health Research Group ( https://www.climatebristol.org/research/climate-and-health-impacts/ ). What will you be doing?: The successful candidates will be responsible for analysing weather forecast, climate, and human health/healthcare data, running weather-health models, and generating forecasts and projections. They will take a lead role in collaborating with external project partners, including ensuring that scientific outputs are useful for decision making in partner organisations. The expectation is that they will lead 1-2 papers peer-reviewed paper each per year. The post-holders will disseminate research findings in meetings and conferences, and engage in outreach or media activities, where applicable. The successful candidates will support one another, the Futureproof project manager, as well as other group members in the wider Climate and Health Research Group. You should apply if: You have a PhD or post-doctoral research experience in meteorology or climate and health, and if you are interested in using your expertise to deliver novel and impactful science. We are looking for quantitative researchers who enjoy working at the intersection of disciplines, particularly with data scientists, epidemiologists, and policymakers from government organisations. We are looking for candidates with a track record in analysing weather, climate, and/or health data, who have strong Python and R programming skills, and who are experienced in or keen on publishing high-quality papers. Additional information: To find out more about what it's like to work in the Faculty of Engineering, and how the Faculty supports people to achieve their potential, please see our staff blog: https://engineering.blogs.bristol.ac.uk/category/engineering-includes-me/ Contract type: Open ended with fixed funding for 3 years (start date between Jan and March 2027) Work pattern: Full time/35 hours per week/100% time Grade: I or J/Pathway 2 Salary: Grade I: £40,704 - £45,641 or Grade J: £45,641 - £51,258 per annum depending on experience School/Unit: School of Engineering, Mathematics and Technology This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on Wednesday 25th November 2026 For informal queries please contact: Dr Eunice Lo, Senior Research Fellow Email: eunice.lo@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    },
    "https://www.bristol.ac.uk/jobs/find/details/?jobId=392040": {
      "title": "Residential Facilities Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Department: Campus Division Reference: SUPP113893 The role: The Residential Facilities Assistant will work across the University's residential properties, providing essential operational support to help maintain a safe, secure, and welcoming environment for students, staff, and visitors. Key responsibilities include managing incoming post and deliveries, supporting resident access and responding to lockouts, undertaking essential compliance activities such as fire alarm testing and fire door inspections, and assisting with waste management operations, including the movement of bins, recycling, and furniture. The role will also involve monitoring and enforcing parking regulations across residential sites, helping to ensure car parks are used safely and appropriately. Working as part of a wider Residential Facilities team, the post holder will liaise with residents, contractors, and colleagues to address operational issues, maintain standards, and support the smooth day-to-day running of the University's accommodation portfolio. The role operates on a rotating shift pattern to provide support across the residential estate. Shifts are worked on a two-week cycle: Week 1 : 7:00am - 2:30pm Week 2 : 11:30am - 7:00pm The ideal candidate will be customer-focused, proactive, and organised, with the ability to work independently, prioritise tasks effectively, and respond professionally to a varied workload in a busy residential environment. Flexibility to work the rotating shift pattern is essential. What will you be doing?: You will work across the University of Bristol’s residential properties, providing front-line facilities and operational support to help ensure a safe, secure, and welcoming environment for students, staff, and visitors. Acting as a key point of contact for residents, you will manage a wide range of enquiries and support the smooth day-to-day operation of the residences. Responsibilities include providing a professional reception service, assisting with arrivals and departures, handling lost property, coordinating deliveries, supporting contractors and visitors, and maintaining positive relationships with residents and guests. The role will also involve managing incoming and outgoing post, supporting accessibility requirements for students, staff, and visitors, and assisting with the delivery of university and external events. A key aspect of the role is the management and issue of keys and access credentials, including responding to resident lockouts, maintaining accurate records, and facilitating access for maintenance, operational, and contractor activities. You will undertake a range of health, safety, and compliance duties, including fire alarm testing, fire door inspections, and other routine building checks. They will identify, report, and where appropriate resolve minor maintenance issues to help maintain high standards across the residential estate. Additional responsibilities include supporting waste management operations, coordinating the movement of bins, recycling, and furniture, monitoring and enforcing parking regulations, and ensuring compliance with university policies and procedures. You will be customer-focused, proactive, and organised, with the ability to manage a varied workload while delivering excellent service and maintaining the highest standards of safety, security, and operational effectiveness across the University’s residential portfolio. You should apply if: Experience of working in a customer service environment. You have held a valid UK driving license for more than 2 years. Additional information: Contract type: Open ended Work pattern: Full time - 35 hours Grade: C Salary: £24,479 - £25,179 per annum School/Unit: Campus Division - Residential Facilities This advert will close at 23:59 UK time on: Wednesday 11th November For informal queries please contact: Megan Lamble ( Residential Facilities Operations Coordinator ) on meg.lamble@bristol.ac.uk Our strategy and mission: We recently launched our strategy to 2030 tying together our mission, vision and values."
    }
  },
